rtist's computer to upload the images); web server executes/responds to the request (does the processing/handling of the upload); web server returns confirmation/error of the processing/upload.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct justification from the same list. Incorrect computer = do not award justification.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network performance factors","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A library has a LAN (Local Area Network)."},{"type":"text","value":"A LAN allows access by both wired and wireless devices."},{"type":"text","value":"Users have reported that the network sometimes runs very slowly."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the number of devices using the network at the same time can affect the performance of the network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 3. More devices mean more data is being transmitted at a time. Bandwidth will be split between all the devices. This means less bandwidth for each device. Devices have to wait longer before they can transmit (increased latency). Slower transmission of data / less data can be transmitted at the same time. Central device has to handle more requests. More collisions likely, more data has to be retransmitted.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"More devices = more data being transmitted","marks":1},{"criteria":"Bandwidth split between devices / less bandwidth per device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Slower transmission / increased latency / devices wait longer / more collisions","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network performance factor","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one other factor that can affect the performance of the network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. E.g. Bandwidth, interference, wired/wireless/transmission medium, type/amount of data being transmitted, central hardware performance (e.g. router/switch), error rate, distance between nodes, topology. Do not award number of users.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid factor identified (e.g. bandwidth, interference, transmission medium, hardware performance, distance, topology)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"accessing websites terms","marks":7,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Users can access websites from library computers."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the description of accessing websites using the given list of terms. Not all terms will be used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: 0, 1, 127, 128, 255, 256, Colon, Domain Name Server, Embedded systems, File server, Full stop, Hyphen, Internet protocol, MAC address, Router, Uniform Resource Locator, Web server, Clients"},{"type":"text","value":"A website is hosted on a ____. The computers that access the websites are called ____."},{"type":"text","value":"The user enters the ____ into a web browser. The web browser sends a request to the ____ for the matching IP (Internet Protocol) address. If found the IP address is returned. A request is then sent to this IP address."},{"type":"text","value":"An IPv4 address is made of 4 groups of digits. Each group can be between the denary values ____ and ____. The groups of digits are separated by a ____."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed term. Web server. Clients. Uniform Resource Locator. Domain Name Server. 0. 255 (accept in either order). Full stop.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Web server","marks":1},{"criteria":"Clients","marks":1},{"criteria":"Uniform Resource Locator","marks":1},{"criteria":"Domain Name Server","marks":1},{"criteria":"0 (lower bound of IPv4 group)","marks":1},{"criteria":"255 (upper bound of IPv4 group)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Full stop (separator)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"router tasks","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A network has several routers."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify three tasks carried out by a router."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 3. Receive packets. Forward/send/transmit packets. Maintain a routing table. Identify the most efficient path to the destination. Assign IP addresses to nodes/devices. Convert packets from one protocol to another. Note: question asks for tasks, not the purpose/use of a router.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Task 1 (e.g. receive packets, forward packets, maintain routing table, assign IP addresses)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Task 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Task 3","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"network type identification","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir has a home network that includes two laptop computers, four mobile phones, and two televisions."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the type of network Amir has at home."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: LAN / Local Area Network.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"LAN / Local Area Network identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"switch vs router","marks":6,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The network uses a star topology with a central switch. The switch has an integrated wireless access point (WAP)."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the similarities and differences between a switch and a router."},{"type":"table","headers":["Similarities","Differences"],"rows":[["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per valid point to max 6. Similarities include that both connect devices, receive data, determine the destination, and transmit data. Differences include that a switch uses MAC addresses while a router uses IP addresses, a switch connects nodes/devices on a LAN, and a router connects networks/the internet.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid similarity 1 (e.g. both connect devices / both receive data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid similarity 2 (e.g. both determine destination / both transmit data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 1 (switch uses MAC addresses, router uses IP addresses)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 2 (switch connects nodes/devices, router connects networks/the internet)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 3","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid additional comparison point","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"star topology diagram","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Draw the star topology for Amir's home network. Clearly label each device."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet. Clearly labelled switch. 2 laptops, 4 phones, and 2 TVs. All devices connected to the switch and nothing else. Connections can be wired or identifiable wireless connections.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Switch clearly labelled","marks":1},{"criteria":"2 laptops, 4 phones, and 2 TVs shown","marks":1},{"criteria":"All devices connected only to the switch","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(i)","parent_question":6,"topic":"cloud storage benefits","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve stores her computer program on the cloud whilst working on it."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits to Eve of storing the program on the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per point to max 3. She can access the program from anywhere. She does not need to carry a storage device. Security / backup is likely managed for her, so she does not need to back up the work manually.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Can access the program from anywhere","marks":1},{"criteria":"Does not need to carry a storage device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Security / backup managed for her","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(ii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"cloud storage drawbacks","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve stores her computer program on the cloud whilst working on it."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the drawbacks to Eve of storing the program on the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per point to max 3. If there is no internet access she cannot access her work. Transmission may not be secure, so the work could be intercepted. Security is out of her control, so it may not be backed up or kept safe.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"No internet access means she cannot access the work","marks":1},{"criteria":"Transmission may not be secure / work could be intercepted","marks":1},{"criteria":"Security / backup is out of her control","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_question":7,"topic":"star topology reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why the bakery may use a star network topology for their LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid reason to max 2. It is easy to add a new node or device. Fewer data collisions can occur. If one node or device fails it does not affect the rest of the network. A signal does not need to be transmitted to all computers in the network.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid reason 1 (e.g. easy to add devices / fewer collisions / one failure does not affect others)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid reason 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)","parent_question":8,"topic":"WAN definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A law company currently use a Local Area Network (LAN) linked to a Wide Area Network (WAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"They want to upgrade their system to utilise cloud storage."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a Wide Area Network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a correct definition, such as computers being geographically remote / separated over a large area, or communication media that is not owned by the law company.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Computers are geographically remote / over a large area, or the communication medium is not owned by the company","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(b)","parent_question":8,"topic":"cloud storage advantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two advantages to the law company of storing their data in the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Two contextualised advantages, worth up to 2 marks each. Advantages include extra storage so the company can take on more cases, efficient backup saving time and money, remote access for employees, reduced need for specialist network skills, third-party security/backup, or lower infrastructure costs. Each advantage must be linked to the law company for full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Advantage 1 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 1 contextualised to the law company","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 2 contextualised to the law company","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(c)","parent_question":8,"topic":"cloud storage disadvantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two disadvantages to the law company of storing their data in the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Two contextualised disadvantages, worth up to 2 marks each. Disadvantages include needing a constant internet connection, reliance on a third party for security or backup, data being vulnerable to hacking, uncertainty over data ownership, and data protection implications. Each disadvantage must be linked to the law company for full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Disadvantage 1 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 1 contextualised to the law company","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 2 contextualised to the law company","marks":1}]}}]`),Zx={paper_id:Ux,qualification:Wx,component:Vx,title:Gx,year:Yx,session:Kx,total_marks:Xx,questions:Qx},e7="J277-TOPIC-1.3.2",t7="J277",a7="TOPIC",r7="Wired and Wireless Networks, Protocols and Layers",i7=null,n7=null,s7=50,o7=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"Wi-Fi connection benefits","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the benefits to the youth centre of also providing Wi-Fi connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 3 e.g. Easier to add more devices/users Can have more devices/connections/users // more scalable … without cost of purchase/install additional wired connections Devices are not fixed to one spot // devices can be moved // can connect from anywhere in (wireless) range …allowing people to use them where they are needed // can use where there is no physical connection Reduces risk of (additional) trailing wires Less potential damage to equipment/wires Connect wider range / more types of device // by example e.g. mobile phones/tablets … that don’t have ports / wired connections // that only have wireless connections It allows a backup network if the wired network fails (Due to each node acting as a repeater in the mesh) the network will have a greater range MP1 is for easier to add more devices to the network. MP2 is there can be more connections/nodes/devices than on a wired. ‘more devices can be added easily’ is MP1 on its own.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid wireless benefit from: easier to add more devices/users (more devices/connections, more scalable, no cost of installing additional wired connections); devices not fixed to one spot (can be moved, used anywhere in wireless range, used where there is no physical connection); reduces risk of trailing wires, less potential damage to equipment/wires; connects wider range/more types of device (e.g. mobile phones/tablets without ports); allows a backup network if wired network fails; greater range (each node acts as a repeater in a mesh).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct benefit from the same list. ('More devices can be added easily' covers MP1 alone; MP2 is the separate point about more devices/connections than wired.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(a)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"network performance factors","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The decision to use wired and/or wireless connections can affect the performance of the network."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by explaining how each factor can affect the performance of a network."},{"type":"table","headers":["Factor","How it affects performance"],"rows":[["Bandwidth",""],["Number of users accessing the network at the same time",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"per bullet to max 2 for each factor Factor How it affects the performance Bandwidth More bandwidth = faster network/transmission BOD better performance …because more data is transferred simultaneously/per second Number of users accessing the network at the same time More users = slower network/transmission BOD worse performance …because more data is transferred simultaneously // more traffic // the bandwidth is being shared/used // more bandwidth used // BOD more congestion / collisions Allow reverse of each. Do not award references to Internet speed alone. Points need to reference more/less data in correct context.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Bandwidth: Award 1 mark for: more bandwidth = faster network/transmission (BOD better performance). Allow reverse. Do not award references to internet speed alone.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Bandwidth: Award 1 mark for the linked reason: ...because more data is transferred simultaneously / per second.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Number of users: Award 1 mark for: more users = slower network/transmission (BOD worse performance). Allow reverse.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Number of users: Award 1 mark for the linked reason: ...because more data is transferred simultaneously / more traffic / bandwidth is shared / more congestion / more collisions.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"internet connection device","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one device that will be required to allow the LAN to connect to the internet."}],"answer":{"guidance":"for router/modem","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"for router/modem","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"IPv4 address validation","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The computers will be allocated an IPv4 address."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box on each row to identify if the IPv4 address is valid or invalid."},{"type":"table","headers":["IPv4 address","Valid (✓)","Invalid (✓)"],"rows":[["192.154.21.2","",""],["258.0.0.3","",""],["56.1.2.66.1","",""],["251.58.3.7","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"for each row. IPv4 address Valid (✓) Invalid (✓) 192.154.21.2 ✓ 258.0.0.3 ✓ 56.1.2.66.1 ✓ 251.58.3.7 ✓ Do not award rows with 2 ticks. Allow Xs in place of ticks in either/both columns.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (192.154.21.2): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Allow X in place of tick.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (258.0.0.3): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (first octet 258 is greater than 255).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (56.1.2.66.1): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (5 octets — IPv4 must have exactly 4).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (251.58.3.7): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Do not award rows with 2 ticks.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"DNS resolution steps","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"When a user wants to access a website they enter the URL into the address bar of a web browser."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps that take place to convert the URL into the website’s IP address."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 4 Browser checks cache for matching IP address URL/domain is sent to DNS DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP …in table/database/files/lists (of URL/domain and IPs) If not found the request is transferred to higher-level DNS DNS returns IP address to computer/browser An error is returned/sent if the IP address cannot be found MP3 – award for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP MP5 accept another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own. Accept: another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for any one of: browser checks cache for matching IP address; URL/domain is sent to DNS.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP (in a table/database/files/lists). MP3 awarded for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: if not found, the request is transferred to a higher-level DNS (accept: another/larger DNS).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS returns IP address to computer/browser (do not award 'user' on its own); OR an error is returned/sent if the IP cannot be found.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"IP address examples","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one valid example of an IPv4 address and one valid example of an IPv6 address."},{"type":"text","value":"IPv4 IPv6"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid IP. v4: 4 groups of denary numbers between 0 and 255 separated by full stops (example v4: 123.16.46.72). v6: 8 groups of hex numbers between 0 and FFFF separated by colons. Double colon can appear once and replaces any number of groups of consecutive 0000 (example v6: 0252:5985:89ab:cdde:a57f:89ad:efcd:00fe) (example v6: F513:8C:2A::999:0000 expanded would be F513:8C:2A:0000:0000:0000:999:0000). Each hex number can be between 1 and 4 digits.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a valid IPv4 example: 4 groups of denary numbers between 0 and 255 