uce delays at check-in', 'so data about passengers/staff/aeroplanes is not intercepted', 'flight status received without delay', 'airport has a large floor area/terminals'). If expansion is not applied to the airport, annotate ^ (counts toward the max-3 cap).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Answer space 2 - benefit: Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid benefit from the same list as space 1.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Answer space 2 - application: Award 1 mark for a matching expansion/application contextualised to the airport. Max 2 marks per answer space; max 3 marks total if expansions have no direct airport application.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"wireless network reasons","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the reasons why the airport should also allow the network to be accessed using a wireless connection."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 3 e.g. Staff do not need to be in one-place // movement of staff // can work whilst moving to another part of the airport // can be accessed from any location (in range) Staff can be more responsive to customers/requests Allows a larger number of connections/devices // more scalable … … without the disruption/cost of installing more cables Some devices do not allow physical/wired connection // allow wider range of type of device (or by example such as vehicles/mobile devices/aeroplanes) Easier to add/connect more devices Do not need to find/use a physical connection/wire // can allow you to connect in a place where there isn't a cable/connection For use as a backup if the wired connection fails Do not award cost on its own. Do not award range on its own. Allow explanation of how a wireless network will benefit the passenger as well as the airport and staff. Allow in reverse if clear, for example wired restricts staff to one location.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid reason from: staff not fixed to one place / movement of staff / can work whilst moving / can be accessed from any location in range; staff can be more responsive to customers/requests; allows a larger number of connections/devices / more scalable (without disruption/cost of installing more cables); some devices do not allow physical/wired connection / wider range of device types (e.g. vehicles, mobile devices, aeroplanes); easier to add/connect more devices; do not need to find/use a physical connection / can connect where there is no cable; for use as a backup if wired connection fails. Do not award 'cost' or 'range' on their own.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid reason from the same list. Allow explanations of how wireless benefits passengers as well as airport/staff. Allow reverse if clear (e.g. 'wired restricts staff to one location').","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct valid reason from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"network protocols","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by identifying the most appropriate protocol for each of the tasks the student is performing."},{"type":"table","headers":["Task","Protocol"],"rows":[["Requesting to view a news webpage from a web server",""],["Entering a username and password to access their bank\\naccount",""],["Downloading a text document from a web server",""],["Checking for new emails in their inbox",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"for each protocol Task Protocol Requesting a webpage from a web server HTTP // HTTPS Entering a username and password to access their bank account HTTPS Downloading a text document from a web server FTP // HTTP // HTTPS Checking for new emails in their inbox IMAP // POP Mark first answer in each box. Allow full name to be written e.g. file transfer (protocol). Accept: POP3 or any other version","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (requesting a webpage from a web server): Award 1 mark for HTTP or HTTPS. Mark first answer in the box. Allow full name (e.g. 'hypertext transfer protocol').","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (entering a username and password to access bank account): Award 1 mark for HTTPS only.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (downloading a text document from a web server): Award 1 mark for FTP, HTTP, or HTTPS. Allow 'file transfer protocol'.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (checking for new emails in inbox): Award 1 mark for IMAP or POP. Accept POP3 or any other version.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"protocol layers","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Some protocols have layers."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why protocols have layers."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2: Each layer is independent // layers are not reliant on other layers One layer can be changed without affecting the others // a layer can function without needing/changing/impacting any other layer // self-contained Separates tasks so they can be developed independently A developer can focus on only one layer // developer can specialise Allows for standards for individual tasks/layers to be developed // for compatibility Manufacturers can develop hardware to fit into one particular layer To group together similar protocols Max 1 in each answer space","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid reason from: each layer is independent / not reliant on others; one layer can be changed without affecting others (self-contained); separates tasks so they can be developed independently; a developer can focus on/specialise in one layer; allows standards for individual layers (compatibility); manufacturers can develop hardware to fit one layer; groups together similar protocols. Max 1 mark in each answer space.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid reason from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"LAN characteristics","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"One characteristic of a LAN is that they are set up over a small geographical area."},{"type":"text","value":"Give one other characteristic of a LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"wireless LAN benefits","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits of the student changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 4: e.g. Allows more devices to connect … …for example televisions, mobile phones Easy to connect (devices) // Easier to setup (wireless connections) // By example e.g. easier for guests to connect their devices Home is likely small area … so short distance wireless is sufficient Devices can move around // can use devices in different areas // can connect from anywhere in the house // can use where wires don't reach // can access from a larger area (than wired) … by example e.g. student is using a laptop so does not need to be tied to one place // by example e.g. they don't have to disconnect before moving // e.g. they can stay connected whilst moving Cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices // no cost for (new/replacement) wires/hardware …because no additional/fewer wires are needed Fewer trip hazards from trailing wires // reduce risk of damage to cables // fewer cables to damage More compatible // some devices only have wireless connections Easier/cheaper on their own is NE","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid benefit from: allows more devices to connect (e.g. televisions, mobile phones); easy to connect/setup wireless devices (e.g. easier for guests); home is likely a small area, so short-distance wireless is sufficient; devices can move around / connect from anywhere in the house / used where wires don't reach; cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices (no cost for replacement wires); fewer trip hazards / less cable damage; more compatible - some devices only have wireless connections. ('Easier'/'cheaper' alone are NE.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a fourth distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(iii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"wireless LAN drawbacks","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State two drawbacks of changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2: e.g. Prone to interference // by example Limited range of signal Slower rate of transmission // less bandwidth // reduced network performance// increased latency // BOD slower connection // more users reduces rate of transmission / bandwidth /performance etc. Increased risk of security concerns // by example e.g. A hacker could connect to the wireless connection Less stable connection Higher chance of collisions // Higher error rate MP3 needs to say what is slower / decreased e.g. It's slower, is NE Mark first drawback in each answer space. Less reliable is TV on its own for MP 5","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid drawback from: prone to interference (or by example); limited range of signal; slower rate of transmission / less bandwidth / reduced network performance / increased latency (must specify what is slower - 'it's slower' alone is NE); increased risk of security concerns (e.g. hacker could connect to wireless); less stable connection; higher chance of collisions / higher error rate. Mark first drawback in each answer space. 'Less reliable' on its own is TV.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"Ethernet as a standard","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Ethernet is considered a standard."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why Ethernet is a standard."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Ethernet is used by (mostly) all manufacturers / used in many devices. To allow compatibility with other devices. Has high bandwidth. Has inbuilt security. Is a proven/reliable connection. Is low cost for purchase/installation/maintenance.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ethernet is widely used / used by most manufacturers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Allows compatibility between devices, OR has specific benefits (high bandwidth, reliable, low cost)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"encryption reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A library does not use encryption when data is transmitted through its network."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why the library should use encryption."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Data cannot be understood if intercepted / data will be meaningless. Only authorised users can access confidential material / protects personal/user/library data. To follow legislation/DPA. Note: question is about transmission, not storage.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reason 1 (e.g. data meaningless if intercepted, protects confidential data, follows legislation)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Reason 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network protocols","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Protocols are used to transmit data through the network and over the internet."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one protocol that can be used to perform each of the following tasks:"},{"type":"text","value":"Send an email: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Access a website securely: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each. Send email: SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol). Access website securely: HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure). Mark first answer in each line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Send email: SMTP","marks":1},{"criteria":"Access website securely: HTTPS","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"network protocol definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The owners of a large bakery have a Local Area Network (LAN) with a star topology. They order their supplies over the Internet. When data is transmitted from the bakery to the supplier, network protocols are used."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a network protocol."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: a set of rules for communication.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A set of rules for communication","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"protocol layer definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"TCP/IP is a set of protocols based on layers."},{"type":"text","value":"With regards to network protocols, define what is meant by a layer."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: a division of network functionality.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A division of network functionality","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"protocol layer advantage","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"TCP/IP is a set of protocols based on layers."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one advantage of using layers to construct network protocols."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for identifying an advantage and 1 mark for description. A layer is self-contained, allowing different developers to focus on one aspect of the network. A layer can be edited or replaced without affecting other layers, promoting interoperability between vendors and systems.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Layers are self-contained / can be worked on separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"A layer can be changed without affecting others / promotes interoperability","marks":1}]}}]`),c7={paper_id:e7,qualification:t7,component:a7,title:r7,year:i7,session:n7,total_marks:s7,questions:o7},l7="J277-TOPIC-1.4",d7="J277",u7="TOPIC",p7="Network Security",m7=null,h7=null,f7=26,g7=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"characteristics of a LAN","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student's computer is part of their home Local Area Network (LAN). The LAN currently only has wired connections."},{"type":"text","value":"One characteristic of a LAN is that they are set up over a small geographical area."},{"type":"text","value":"Give one other characteristic of a LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid characteristic given (e.g. uses own/internal hardware, no third-party infrastructure, devices use MAC addresses)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"benefits of wireless LAN","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits of the student changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 4. e.g. Allows more devices to connect (e.g. televisions, mobile phones). Easier to connect/setup (e.g. easier for guests to connect). Home is likely a small area so short distance wireless is sufficient. Devices can move around / can connect from anywhere in the house / can use where wires don't reach (e.g. student using a laptop does not need to be tied to one place). Cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices because no additional/fewer wires are needed. Fewer trip hazards from trailing wires / fewer cables to damage. More compatible — some devices only have wireless connections. 'Easier'/'cheaper' on their own is NE.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Benefit 1 (e.g. more/wider range of devices can connect)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 2 (e.g. easier to connect/setup, or devices can move around the house)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 3 (e.g. cheaper since fewer wires needed, fewer trip hazards)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 4 (e.g. more compatibility with wireless-only devices)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"drawbacks of wireless LAN","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State two drawbacks of changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."},{"type":"text","value":"1. ____"},{"type":"text","value":"2. ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. e.g. Prone to interference. Limited range of signal. Slower rate of transmission / less bandwidth / reduced network performance / increased latency (must specify what is slower — 'it's slower' is NE). Increased risk of security concerns (e.g. a hacker could connect to the wireless connection). Less stable connection. Higher chance of collisions / higher error rate. Mark first drawback in each answer space. 'Less reliable' on its own is TV.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Drawback 1 (e.g. interference, limited range, slower transmission, security risk, less stable, more collisions)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Drawback 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"threat prevention methods","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one or more boxes on each row to identify all of the methods that can help to prevent each threat."},{"type":"table","headers":["Threat","Anti-malware","Penetration testing","Encryption","Firewall"],"rows":[["Spyware","","","",""],["Brute-force attack","","","",""],["Data interception","","","",""],["SQL injection","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per row. Each row has a primary required tick (✓) and may have additional optional ticks ((✓)) that are accepted but not required. Award the mark if the primary tick is present; do not penalise extra optional ticks or their absence. Spyware: required = Anti-malware; optional = Firewall. Brute-force attack: required = Firewall; optional = Penetration testing. Data interception: required = Encryption only (firewall and penetration testing do NOT prevent data interception in transit — do not award them). SQL injection: required = Penetration testing; optional = Firewall.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Spyware row: Anti-malware ticked (required). Firewall accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Penetration testing, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Brute-force attack row: Firewall ticked (required). Penetration testing accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Anti-malware, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Data interception row: Encryption ticked (required, sole correct answer). Any other tick (Anti-malware, Penetration testing, Firewall) is wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"SQL injection row: Penetration testing ticked (required). Firewall accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Anti-malware, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"other computer system threats","marks":3,"command_word":"name and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Name and describe one threat to a computer system that is not Spyware, Brute Force attack, Data Interception or SQL Injection."},{"type":"text","value":"Threat: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Description: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for threat, 1 mark each to max 2 for description. Valid threats include: Social engineering (deception to manipulate users to gain personal data); Shoulder surfing (watching a person enter a password, then using it); Phishing (fake emails / clicking link in fake email; person gives away personal data); Pharming (software/fake website that redirects user; person enters personal data); Denial of Service / DOS / DDOS (multiple/flood of requests to a server; more than server can process / uses all bandwidth; server cannot respond / crashes / denies access); Hacker (unauthorised access to system/account; to delete/damage/access data); Virus/Malware (software that replicates/spreads; fills disk space; deletes/corrupts data / allows unauthorised access); Trojan (malware disguised as legitimate software; once installed acts as a virus); Worm (software that replicates across a network; uses up all bandwidth); Ransomware (encrypts/locks access to data; cannot access without paying a fee — 'ransom' alone is NE); Physical threat (damage to hardware; deletes/corrupts data). If threat is clearly wrong, do not FT.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid threat correctly named (not spyware, brute-force, data interception, or SQL injection)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description point 1 matching the threat","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description point 2 matching the threat","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3","parent_question":3,"topic":"forms of attack and prevention","marks":6,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir has a home network that includes two laptop computers, four mobile phones, and two televisions. Amir wants to protect the computers on his network from threats such as unauthorised access."},{"type":"text","value":"The following incomplete table contains a form of attack, description and method of preventing each attack. Complete the table by writing the missing Forms of attack, Descriptions and Methods of prevention."},{"type":"table","headers":["Form of attack","Description of attack","Method of prevention"],"rows":[["","A program attempting all possible password combinations",""],["Data interception","",""],["","","Anti-virus"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed box (6 total). Row 1: Form = Brute-force attack; Prevention = Strong password / set number of password attempts / firewall. Row 2: Description = Data transmission being read by unauthorised user/program; Prevention = Encryption. Row 3: Form = Malware / Virus / Trojan etc.; Description = Software that damages/deletes data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 form: Brute-force attack","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 1 prevention: Strong password / limit password attempts / firewall","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 description: Data transmission being read by unauthorised user/program","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 prevention: Encryption","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 form: Malware / Virus / Trojan","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 description: Software that damages/deletes data","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"human errors and preventative procedures","marks":6,"command_word":"identify and outline","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hospital stores patients' details on its computer network. The hospital is concerned about the security of its patients' details."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify three errors that the hospital staff could make that may endanger the security of the network. Outline a procedure that could be put in place to prevent each error."},{"type":"text","value":"Error 1: ____\\nProcedure 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Error 2: ____\\nProcedure 2: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Error 3: ____\\nProcedure 3: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct identification (max 3, AO2 1a) and 1 mark for each correct procedure (max 3, AO2 1b). Allow any reasonable combination of error and procedure. Examples: Bringing in files via any medium → not allowing/stopping external devices being used on the network. Downloading infected files from the internet → blocking/restricting access to insecure websites. Allowing physical access to the surgery's network → locking of doors / key cards / physical security procedure. Sending/sharing sensitive data with third parties → blocking/restricting access to USB ports / email / internet / printing.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Error 1 identified (e.g. brings in files via external media, downloads infected files, allows physical access, shares sensitive data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 1 mitigates Error 1 (e.g. block external devices, restrict insecure sites, lock doors/key cards, restrict USB/email)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Error 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 2 mitigates Error 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Error 3 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 3 mitigates Error 3","marks":1}]}}]`),_7={paper_id:l7,qualification:d7,component:u7,title:p7,year:m7,session:h7,total_marks:f7,questions:g7},y7="J277-TOPIC-1.5.1",w7="J277",v7="TOPIC",b7="Operating Systems",k7=null,x7=null,A7=14,q7=[{question_id:"1.5.1-1",parent_question:1,topic:"operating system functions",marks:5,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Tick one box on each row to identify the computer operating system function that will manage each task."},{type:"table",headers:["Operating system function","Memory management","Peripheral management","User management","File management"],rows:[["Multitasking","","","",""],["Renaming a folder","","","",""],["Creation of user accounts","","","",""],["Installation of a printer driver","","","",""],["Transfer of data to and from RAM","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each correct row. Multitasking and transferring data to and from RAM are managed by memory management. Renaming a folder is file management. Creating user accounts is user management. Installing a printer driver is peripheral management.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Multitasking = memory management",marks:1},{criteria:"Renaming a folder = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Creation of user accounts = user management",marks:1},{criteria:"Installation of a printer driver = peripheral management",marks:1},{criteria:"Transfer of data to and from RAM = memory management",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.1-2",parent_question:2,topic:"operating system functions",marks:4,command_word:"complete",content:[{type:"text",value:"The table contains operating system functions and a task that each function performs."},{type:"text",value:"Complete the table by writing the two missing function names and a task performed by the two given functions."},{type:"table",headers:["Function","Task"],rows:[["","Moves data from secondary storage to RAM"],["Peripheral management",""],["","Allows the user to create, name and delete folders"],["User interface",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each missing function or task. The first function is memory management. A valid peripheral-management task includes receiving data from input devices, sending data to output devices, using device drivers, or enabling communication with peripherals. The third function is file management. A valid user-interface task includes receiving input from the user, outputting data to the user, or allowing the user to interact with a GUI or command prompt.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Memory management identified",marks:1},{criteria:"Valid peripheral-management task described",marks:1},{criteria:"File management identified",marks:1},{criteria:"Valid user-interface task described",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.1-3",parent_question:3,topic:"operating system functions",marks:5,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{type:"text",value:"Tick one box in each row to identify which function of the Operating System deals with each action."},{type:"table",headers:["Action","Memory management","Peripheral management","File management","User management"],rows:[["Creating a new folder to store documents in","","","",""],["Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM","","","",""],["Renaming a file","","","",""],["Reading data from a scanner","","","",""],["Changing the password required to log on to the computer","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each correct row. Creating a new folder and renaming a file are file-management actions. Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM is memory management. Reading data from a scanner is peripheral management. Changing the logon password is user management. No mark should be awarded for a row with more than one box ticked.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Creating a new folder to store documents in = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM = memory management",marks:1},{criteria:"Renaming a file = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Reading data from a scanner = peripheral management",marks:1},{criteria:"Changing the password required to log on to the computer = user management",marks:1}]}}],C7={paper_id:y7,qualification:w7,component:v7,title:b7,year:k7,session:x7,total_marks:A7,questions:q7},S7="J277-TOPIC-1.5.2",T7="J277",P7="TOPIC",j7="Utility Software",M7=null,I7=null,E7=21,N7=[{question_id:"1.5.2-1(i)",parent_question:1,topic:"utility software purpose",marks:1,command_word:"state",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer has utility software."},{type:"text",value:"State the purpose of utility software in a computer."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark. Utility software performs housekeeping or maintenance tasks, helps the computer run smoothly, or monitors, manages, configures, diagnoses, or fixes the system. Do not award a specific example on its own.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Valid general purpose of utility software stated",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-1(ii)",parent_question:1,topic:"encryption software",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer has utility software."},{type:"text",value:"Explain the purpose and function of encryption software."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to max 3. Encryption software scrambles or encodes data. It uses an algorithm or key to encrypt and decrypt the data. If the encrypted data is intercepted or accessed without authorisation, it is meaningless and cannot be understood.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Data is scrambled, encoded, or turned into ciphertext",marks:1},{criteria:"An algorithm or key is used to encrypt and decrypt the data",marks:1},{criteria:"Encrypted data is meaningless if intercepted or accessed without authorisation",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-2",parent_question:2,topic:"utility software description",marks:6,command_word:"complete",content:[{type:"text",value:"Complete the description of utility system software using the words provided in the box. Not all words are used."},{type:"text",value:"Terms: access, amount, apart, compression, consecutive, defragmentation, deleted, encryption, key, lock, quantity, separate, speed, understood"},{type:"text",value:"____ software changes data using a ____. If the changed data is intercepted, it cannot be ____. This software does not stop the data from being intercepted. ____ software analyses the data on a disk to find files that have been split and stored in separate locations. The split files are moved to be ____ in storage and the free space is moved together. This does not provide more storage space on the disk, instead it makes the ____ of the data faster because the read head does not have to move as far to access the next part of the file."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each completed term. Encryption software changes data using a key. If the changed data is intercepted, it cannot be understood. This software does not stop the data from being intercepted. Defragmentation software analyses the data on a disk to find files that have been split and stored in separate locations. The split files are moved to be consecutive in storage, making access to the data faster.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Encryption",marks:1},{criteria:"Key",marks:1},{criteria:"Understood",marks:1},{criteria:"Defragmentation",marks:1},{criteria:"Consecutive",marks:1},{criteria:"Access",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-3",parent_question:3,topic:"utility software purpose",marks:1,command_word:"state",content:[{type:"text",value:"The computer has an operating system and utility software."},{type:"text",value:"State the need for utility software in a computer."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark. Utility software performs housekeeping tasks, helps to monitor, manage, maintain, or diagnose the system, or helps the computer run smoothly and efficiently. Do not award an example on its own.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Valid need for utility software stated",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-4",parent_question:4,topic:"defragmentation benefits",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{type:"text",value:"Eve runs the defragmentation program on her computer."},{type:"text",value:"Explain why Eve's computer is more efficient after the program has been run."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 3. Files are stored contiguously, so they take less time to read or access. The read head does not need to move as far to reach the next piece of data. Free space is grouped together, so saving new files is faster and less likely to split them up.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Files are contiguous so reading or accessing files is faster",marks:1},{criteria:"The read head does not need to move as far to read the next part of a file",marks:1},{criteria:"Free space is grouped together so saving new files is faster or less likely to fragment them",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-5(a)",parent_question:5,topic:"disk fragmentation cause",marks:4,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A restaurant has a computer-based ordering system which is running slowly."},{type:"text",value:"A technician has said that the hard disc drive is fragmented. The technician has suggested using utility software to defragment the drive."},{type:"text",value:"Explain how the restaurant's hard disc could have become fragmented."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 4. Order files are saved to the system and then deleted once processed. New order files are created later. Some new files are larger than the gaps left by deleted files. Those files are split across separate locations on the disk.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Order files are saved and then deleted once processed",marks:1},{criteria:"New order files are created later",marks:1},{criteria:"Some new files are larger than the empty spaces left behind",marks:1},{criteria:"Files are split across the disk into separate locations",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-5(b)",parent_question:5,topic:"defragmentation software",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A restaurant has a computer-based ordering system which is running slowly."},{type:"text",value:"A technician has said that the hard disc drive is fragmented. The technician has suggested using utility software to defragment the drive."},{type:"text",value:"Explain how defragmentation software could overcome the issue of the slow computer system."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 3. Files on the hard disk are moved. Empty spaces are collected together. Files are stored together, so fewer disk accesses are needed when they are read.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Files on the hard disk are moved",marks:1},{criteria:"Empty spaces are collected together",marks:1},{criteria:"Files are stored together so fewer disk accesses are needed",marks:1}]}}],R7={paper_id:S7,qualification:T7,component:P7,title:j7,year:M7,session:I7,total_marks:E7,questions:N7},L7="J277-TOPIC-1.6.1",J7="J277",O7="TOPIC",D7="Ethical, Legal, Cultural and Environmental Impact",B7=null,F7=null,$7=74,z7=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1.6.1-1","parent_question":1,"topic":"tablet computer upgrade impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A company designs and builds tablet computers. The company wants to reduce the cost of manufacturing their tablet computers."},{"type":"text","value":"The company also decides to stop releasing software updates for their older tablet computers. This means customers will need to change their devices more often."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of these decisions on the company and the customers."},{"type":"text","value":"- ethical issues\\n- environmental issues\\n- benefits and drawbacks"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Top-band responses include developed benefits and drawbacks, at least one ethical issue and one environmental issue, and clear reference to both the company and the customers. Indicative content includes increased profit or sales for the company, lower purchase cost for some customers, possible damage to reputation, customers being forced to replace devices more often, more e-waste, recycling or reuse of old devices, fairness to customers, and whether customers are clearly told about the decision.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about benefit, drawback, ethical, or environmental impact with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some required areas with some linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion covering benefits and drawbacks with ethical and environmental issues, linked to both company and customers","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-2","parent_question":2,"topic":"data protection legislation","marks":4,"command_word":"identify and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A youth centre is creating a network with 20 computers in a single building that will be connected in a Local Area Network (LAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one piece of legislation that the youth centre will need to follow when storing users' data and describe the steps they need to take to meet the requirements of this legislation."},{"type":"table","headers":["Section","Your answer"],"rows":[["Legislation",""],["Steps the youth centre needs to follow",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming the Data Protection Act 2018, DPA, GDPR, or data-protection legislation. 1 mark each to max 3 for valid steps such as keeping data secure, not sharing data without consent, only collecting relevant data, keeping it accurate and up to date, deleting it when no longer needed, only using it for the stated purpose, or allowing users to access their data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Data Protection Act 2018, DPA, GDPR, or equivalent data-protection legislation identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First valid compliance step described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid compliance step described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third valid compliance step described","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-3","parent_question":3,"topic":"open source vs proprietary","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computer programmer has developed a computer game that they want to release for users to download over the internet."},{"type":"text","value":"The programmer needs to decide whether to release the game as open source or proprietary software."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the features, benefits and drawbacks of each type of licence for this program and make a recommendation to the programmer."},{"type":"text","value":"- features of each licence\\n- legal and ethical issues of each licence\\n- benefits and drawbacks of each licence"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Top-band responses cover both open source and proprietary software, address legal and ethical issues, benefits and drawbacks, and give a justified recommendation. Indicative content includes open source usually being free with editable source code and redistribution rights, proprietary software being sold with restricted access to the code, copyright applying to both, wider access and easier bug fixing for open source, limited financial gain for open source, and greater profit and control for proprietary software.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic facts about open source and/or proprietary software","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some required features, issues, benefits, or drawbacks","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed comparison of both licence types with justified recommendation","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-4(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"proprietary software benefits","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An artist is working with a programmer on the development of a new piece of software."},{"type":"text","value":"The software will allow users to edit images on devices such as mobile telephones. They are considering releasing the software as open source instead of proprietary."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two benefits to the artist and programmer of releasing the software as proprietary."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for valid benefits or linked developments. Valid points include earning money from sales or licences, preventing users from seeing or editing the source code, reducing unauthorised copying or reselling, and keeping more control over what users can do with the software. Benefits must be to the artist and programmer, not the user.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Can earn money by selling the software or charging licence fees","marks":1},{"criteria":"Users cannot access or edit the source code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Unauthorised copying, sharing, or reselling is reduced","marks":1},{"criteria":"The creators keep more control over how the software is used","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-4(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"open source benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An artist is working with a programmer on the development of a new piece of software."},{"type":"text","value":"The software will allow users to edit images on devices such as mobile telephones. They are considering releasing the software as open source instead of proprietary."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one benefit to the users of releasing the software as open source."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a valid user benefit and 1 mark for linked development. Valid points include users being able to view or edit the source code, tailor the software to their needs, fix errors, learn how it works, access it for free, or redistribute it with changes.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Users can access or edit the source code, or use the software for free","marks":1},{"criteria":"Linked benefit such as adapting it, fixing errors, learning from it, or redistributing changes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-5","parent_question":5,"topic":"facial recognition impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A shopping centre has a security system that includes CCTV cameras to record activities in the centre."},{"type":"text","value":"The security system is being upgraded to include the use of facial recognition to identify, track the movements of and record individuals throughout the shopping centre."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative impacts of this upgrade including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- ethical issues\\n- privacy issues\\n- legal issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes legal requirements under the Data Protection Act, informing customers, keeping data secure, using evidence to identify criminals, customers feeling safer, people feeling watched or tracked, privacy concerns about storing movement data, and the fact that the centre is private property. Stronger answers give a balanced discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one impact with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion of most required areas with some explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-6","parent_question":6,"topic":"AI and social networking","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"*Social networking websites use artificial intelligence (AI) to monitor posts from users."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative uses of AI by social networking websites including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- legal issues\\n- ethical issues\\n- privacy issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes checking posts for plagiarism or illegal material, complying with data-protection rules, users agreeing through terms and conditions, concerns about users being monitored or posts being blocked incorrectly, safer use of the website, and privacy concerns about users feeling watched. Stronger answers balance positives and negatives across legal, ethical, and privacy issues.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one issue with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion of most required areas with some explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-7","parent_question":7,"topic":"legislation identification","marks":5,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box on each row to identify the legislation that would cover each of the given events."},{"type":"table","headers":["Event","The Data Protection Act (2018)","Computer Misuse Act (1990)","Copyright Designs and Patents Act (1988)"],"rows":[["A company transmits personal data to another company without the individual's permission.","","",""],["A school accidentally publishes their students' addresses on the school website.","","",""],["The interface for a piece of software is replicated by a rival company.","","",""],["A user leaves a computer logged on and another person leaves them a message on their desktop.","","",""],["A student guesses their teacher's password and accesses their computer account.","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct row. Transmitting personal data without permission and publishing student addresses are covered by the Data Protection Act. Replicating a software interface is covered by the Copyright Designs and Patents Act. Leaving a message on another user's logged-in desktop and guessing a password to access an account are covered by the Computer Misuse Act.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Transmitting personal data without permission = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Publishing student addresses = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Replicating a software interface = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Leaving a message on another user's desktop = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Guessing a teacher's password and accessing the account = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-8","parent_question":8,"topic":"copyright legislation","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla is an artist. She draws images by hand. The image is then scanned and stored on a computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Layla wants to protect her images so they cannot be copied by other people."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify which legislation can help protect Layla's images."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. Copyright Designs and Patents Act.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Copyright Designs and Patents Act identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-9(i)","parent_question":9,"topic":"open source licence benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve uses a computer to write a computer game. She wants people to be able to download her program online."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve is choosing between an open source and proprietary licence."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two benefits to the customers of Eve choosing an open source licence."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid benefit. Customers may get the software free of charge, and they can adapt it or add features.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Free of charge","marks":1},{"criteria":"Customers can adapt it or add features","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-9(ii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"proprietary licence benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve uses a computer to write a computer game. She wants people to be able to download her program online."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve is choosing between an open source and proprietary licence."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two benefits to Eve of choosing a proprietary licence."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid benefit. She can charge customers or earn a profit, and she can restrict what users can do with the software so they cannot edit it.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"She can charge customers or earn a profit","marks":1},{"criteria":"She can restrict what users can do or prevent them editing it","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-10","parent_question":10,"topic":"digital technology in medicine","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The use of digital technology in medicine is constantly evolving."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of digital technology on medicine including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- diagnosing patients\\n- treating patients\\n- storage of records"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes diagnosis becoming faster through searching records, pattern finding, or AI-assisted equipment; treatment using automation or remote specialist control; and centralised storage of records being easier for medical staff to access. Drawbacks include incorrect diagnoses, hacking, fatal software errors, reduced human interaction, and privacy or security risks if centralised records are leaked.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about diagnosis, treatment, or record storage","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some of the required areas with linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed discussion across diagnosis, treatment, and record storage with balanced impacts","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-11","parent_question":11,"topic":"legislation identification","marks":6,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A law company currently use a Local Area Network (LAN) linked to a Wide Area Network (WAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"They want to upgrade their system to utilise cloud storage. Fig. 2 lists some actions that may take place in the law company's office."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show which legislation applies to each action."},{"type":"table","headers":["Action","Data Protection Act 2018","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988"],"rows":[["Using a picture for the law company's new logo without the original creator's permission.","","",""],["A secretary accessing a lawyer's personal email account without permission.","","",""],["Making a copy of the latest Hollywood blockbuster movie and sharing it with a client.","","",""],["Storing customer data insecurely.","","",""],["A lawyer installing a key logger on the secretary's computer.","","",""],["Selling client's personal legal data to a marketing company without their permission.","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct row. Using a picture without permission and copying and sharing a movie are covered by the Copyright Designs and Patents Act. Unauthorised access to email and installing a key logger are covered by the Computer Misuse Act. Storing customer data insecurely and selling personal legal data without permission are covered by the Data Protection Act. No mark for a row with more than one tick.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Using a picture without permission = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Accessing a lawyer's email without permission = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Copying and sharing a movie = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Storing customer data insecurely = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Installing a key logger = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Selling personal legal data without permission = Data Protection Act","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-12","parent_question":12,"topic":"smartphone upgrade impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"* People often want to buy the most up-to-date smartphones, even though the smartphone they own still works."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of people wanting to upgrade to the latest smartphone."},{"type":"text","value":"In your answer, you might consider the impact on:\\n- smartphone users\\n- cultural issues\\n- ethical issues\\n- environmental issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes users wanting the newest devices to fit in or gain new features, financial pressure on users or parents, the digital divide, social pressure and possible bullying, devices being designed for short lifespans, e-waste, landfill, toxic waste, wasted precious metals, and the environmental impact of disposal in the UK or abroad.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one impact on users, ethics, culture, or the environment","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some of the required areas with linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed discussion across users, cultural, ethical, and environmental impacts","marks":3}]}}]`),H7={paper_id:L7,qualification:J7,component:O7,title:D7,year:B7,session:F7,total_marks:$7,questions:z7},U7="J277-TOPIC-2.1.1",W7="J277",V7="TOPIC",G7="Computational Thinking",Y7=null,K7=null,X7=61,Q7=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1","parent_question":1,"topic":"computational-thinking-techniques","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Computational thinking techniques were used to design a security system.\\n\\nDraw one line to match each computational thinking technique to its correct example."},{"type":"matching","left_heading":"Computational thinking technique","right_heading":"Example","prompts":[{"id":"abstraction","label":"Abstraction"},{"id":"decomposition","label":"Decomposition"},{"id":"algorithmic-thinking","label":"Algorithmic thinking"}],"options":[{"id":"A","label":"The system is broken down into modules for security and payments."},{"id":"B","label":"There is an increase in the amount of RAM in the computer running the system."},{"id":"C","label":"The user's identity, PIN and password are required. All other data is ignored."},{"id":"D","label":"The step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed."}]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct link for abstraction to the third example ('The user's identity, PIN and password are required. All other data is ignored.'). Correct link for decomposition to the first example ('The system is broken down into modules for security and payments.'). Correct link for algorithmic thinking to the bottom example ('The step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed.'). No mark is awarded for more than one connection from a box.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct link for abstraction to third example (user's identity, PIN and password required; all other data ignored)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct link for decomposition to first example (system is broken down into modules for security and payments)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct link for algorithmic thinking to bottom example (step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-1.png"}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"data-types","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Students take part in a sports day. The students are put into teams.\\n\\nStudents gain points depending on their result and the year group they are in. The points are added to the team score.\\n\\nThe team with the most points at the end of the sports day wins.\\n\\nData about the teams and students is stored in a sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify the most appropriate data type for each variable used by the program.\\n\\nEach data type must be different."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Example","Data type"],"rows":[["teamName","\\"Super-Team\\"",""],["studentYearGroup","11",""],["javelinThrow","18.2",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept alternative equivalent correct data types (e.g. single/double/decimal for BP3). Do not accept char for BP1. Examiner's Comments: This question was completed very well by the vast majority of candidates, showing that the use of data types are now well understood by centres.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"teamName = String","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentYearGroup = Integer","marks":1},{"criteria":"javelinThrow = Real / Float (accept single/double/decimal)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-a-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"linear-search-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student names for a team are stored in an array with the identifier \`theTeam\`.\\n\\nAn example of the data in this array is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Data","Ali","Eve","Ling","Nina","Sarah","Tom"]]},{"type":"text","value":"A linear search function is used to find whether a student is in the team. The function:\\n\\n- takes a student name as a parameter\\n- returns \`True\` if the student name is in the array\\n- returns \`False\` if the student name is not in the array.\\n\\nComplete the design of an algorithm for the linear search function."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"function linearSearch(studentName)\\n   for count = 0 to ____\\n     if theTeam[____] == ____ then\\n       return ____\\n     endif\\n   next count\\n   return False\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept 6 // theTeam.length() for BP1 (Python). Accept alternative length functions e.g. len(). Accept count = 5 (and equivalents) for BP1. Accept \\"True\\" for BP4. Do not allow obvious spaces in variable names. Ignore capitalisation. Examiner's Comments: This was a challenging question revolving around the use of an array that is iterated through to implement a linear search. Many candidates achieved two marks for the first and last points, understanding that the loop would repeat from indexes 0 to 5 and that the value True would be returned if the item was found. As usual, allowance was given for off-by-one errors with this loop because of the prevalence of Python in centres and how count controlled loops are handled in this language. The most challenging mark was certainly the use of count as the index of the theTeam array, with very few candidates correctly identifying this as the missing element.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First blank: theTeam.length() - 1 // 5 (accept 6 // theTeam.length() for Python)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second blank (array index): count","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third blank (comparison value): studentName","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth blank (return value): True","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-003.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-a-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"trace-table","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm calculates the number of points a student gets for the distance they throw in the javelin:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 javelinThrow = input(\\"Enter distance\\")\\n02 yearGroup = input(\\"Enter year group\\")\\n03 if javelinThrow >= 20.0 then\\n04   score = 3\\n05 elseif javelinThrow >= 10.0 then\\n06   score = 2\\n07 else\\n08   score = 1\\n09 endif\\n10 if yearGroup != 11 then\\n11   score = score * 2\\n12 endif\\n13 print(\\"The score is\\", score)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm when a student in year 10 throws a distance of 14.3\\n\\nYou may not need to use all the rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","javelinThrow","yearGroup","score","Output"],"rows":[["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example trace: Line 01: javelinThrow = 14.3. Line 02: yearGroup = 10. Line 06: score = 2. Line 11: score = 4. Line 13: Output \\"The score is 4\\". Answer may include lines where no changes or output happens (i.e. lines 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12). Where variable doesn't change, current value may be repeated on subsequent lines. Max 3 if in wrong order or additional (incorrect) changes. Penalise line numbers once then FT. Allow FT for BP4 for current value of score. BP4 must not include comma. Ignore superfluous spaces. Ignore quotation marks. Treat any entry in output column as an output, even if \\"x\\", \\"-\\" or \\"0\\". Examiner's Comments: Trace tables have appeared multiple times in J277 examination papers now and candidates are hopefully familiar with the expectations, which are consistent across series. Candidates should be encouraged to simply leave boxes blank if no output is given on a particular line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"javelinThrow set to 14.3 on line 01 and yearGroup set to 10 on line 02","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 2 on line 06","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 4 on line 11","marks":1},{"criteria":"\\"The score is 4\\" output on line 13 with no additional outputs (allow input statements)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-003.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-b.png"}},{"question_id":"2(c)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"validation-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated.\\n\\nThe height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \`VALID\` or \`NOT VALID\` depending on the height input.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (checking for valid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nExample 2 (checking for invalid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nAnswers using AND/OR for BP2 and BP3 must be logically correct e.g. if height >=40 and height <=180. Do not accept if height >=40 and <=180. Answers using OR will reverse output for BP4. BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3. Allow FT for BP4 if reasonable attempt at validating (must include at least one boundary). Ignore conversion to int on input. input cannot be used as a variable name. Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for a high-level language / ERL. Do not accept => (wrong way around) or the mathematical >= symbol (not available on keyboard). No obvious spaces in variable names. Penalise once and then FT.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses it","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 // < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 // > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-005.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-c-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(c)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"test-data","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests.\\n\\nComplete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Normal: Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive), e.g. 50. Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0. Erroneous: Any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value. No need to include decimals, e.g. accept 50. Ignore cm if given. Answer must be actual data (e.g. 50) and not description of data (e.g. \\"a value between 40 and 180\\"). If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for BP3.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: any value between 40 and 180 inclusive (e.g. 50)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 or 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: any value less than 40, any value greater than 180, or any non-numeric value","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-005.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-c-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(d)","parent_question":2,"topic":"sql-query","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database.\\n\\nThe database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: SELECT StudentID, TeamName FROM TblResult WHERE YearGroup = 11. Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1. Spelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments). Examiner's Comments: A number of candidates struggled with this question. Problems included spaces in field names, misspelling of the table name (such as TblResults plural when TblResult singular was given) or misunderstanding of the WHERE clause.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE keyword used","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11 condition","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-d.png"}},{"question_id":"2(e)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"abstraction-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design). Examples: 'focus on student names and events', 'ignore data such as students' favourite subjects', 'store year groups instead of ages or DOB', 'shows student IDs instead of full student details'. Must be applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is. Give BOD (benefit of the doubt) where this is unclear. Examiner's Comments: Where the context of the sports day was used, candidates were generally successful in achieving this mark. No marks were given by examiners for generic definitions of what the term abstraction means.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data in the design, applicable to the sports day program context","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-e-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(e)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"decomposition-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or any example of breaking down the database into tables. Examples: 'splits the program up into different events', 'separates the validation routines into subroutines', 'breaks the database down into a table per event'. Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description of what decomposition is. Give BOD where this is unclear. Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields (e.g. split into StudentID, YearGroup, etc). BOD if answer discusses one table but suggests other tables could be used. Do not give answers relating simply to data being split into smaller groups unless this clearly relates to how data is decomposed into tables in the DB. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines OR breaking down the database into tables, applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-e-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(f)","parent_question":2,"topic":"algorithm-design","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score).\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1:\\nhighscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nExample 2 (alternative):\\nscores = []\\nteams = []\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  scores.append(score)\\n  teams.append(team)\\nendwhile\\nhighscore = max[scores]\\nhighteam = teams[scores.index(highscore)]\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nFor BP3, allow \\"stop\\" to be entered for either team name or score (or both). Allow third input (e.g. \\"do you wish to stop?\\"). Allow use of break (or equivalent) to exit loop for BP3. Allow use of recursive function(s) for BP2/3. Initialisation of variables not needed - assume variables are 0 or empty string if not set. Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score, assume only one team has the highest score. BP4/5 could be done in many ways. Allow any logically valid method. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists. FT for BP6 if attempt made at calculating highest score/name. If answer simply asks for multiple entries (not using iteration), BP2 and 3 cannot be accessed but all others available. For minor syntax errors (e.g. missing quotation marks or == for assignment, spaces in variable names) penalise once then FT. input cannot be used as a variable name.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store / use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"Iterates to enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-f.png"}},{"question_id":"3","parent_question":3,"topic":"computational-thinking-definitions","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the description of computational thinking using the given list of terms.\\n\\nNot all terms will be used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: abstraction, algorithm, computation, decomposition, evaluation, flowchart, origin, program, pseudocode, research, sequence, thinking"},{"type":"text","value":"Computational thinking is the process of analysing problems so that they can be solved in a logical way.\\n\\nThe process of ____ breaks down a problem into smaller, more manageable parts.\\n\\nThe process of ____ removes unnecessary detail from the problem, so that the main components can be focused on.\\n\\nAlgorithmic ____ identifies the main steps needed to solve the problem and the ____ that the steps are completed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Answers in order: decomposition, abstraction, thinking, sequence. Ignore minor misspellings.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First blank: decomposition","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second blank: abstraction","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third blank: thinking","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth blank: sequence","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-3.png"}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"algorithm-design-game","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to play a game with the following rules.\\n\\n- the player is asked 3 addition questions\\n- each question asks the player to add together two random whole numbers between 1 and 10 inclusive\\n- if the player gets the correct answer, 1 is added to their score\\n- at the end of the game their score is displayed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 6. Example answer:\\nscore = 0\\nfor count = 1 to 3\\n  num1 = random(1, 10)\\n  num2 = random(1, 10)\\n  ans = input(\\"What is \\" + num1 + \\" + \\" + num2 + \\"?\\")\\n  if ans = num1 + num2 then\\n    score = score + 1\\n  end if\\nnext count\\nprint(\\"You scored \\" + score)\\n\\nNo need to cast data to string/integer. If random numbers chosen, BP3 must use these. If no random numbers chosen, allow manually setting values. BP6 can be awarded for either a loop repeating 3 times or the same code written out 3 times. BP5 can be given FT if sensible attempt at BP4. Do not award BP6 if same numbers used for every question. Must pick new values each time. Do not penalise potential off by 1 errors for looping (Python) or random number generation.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Initialise / declare score (to zero) before use, outside of any loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Generates 2 random numbers between 1 and 10","marks":1},{"criteria":"Inputs answer from user displaying suitable numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if input is correct answer","marks":1},{"criteria":"If correct adds 1 to score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeats BP2 to 5 three times (for bullet points attempted); outputs score after reasonable attempt at counting","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-008.png"],["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-024.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-4.png"}},{"question_id":"5","parent_question":5,"topic":"high-vs-low-level-languages","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table contains four statements about programming languages.\\n\\nTick one box in each row to identify whether each statement describes a low-level programming language or a high-level programming language."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","Low-level","High-level"],"rows":[["The same language can be used on computers that use different hardware","",""],["It allows the user to directly manipulate memory","",""],["It allows the user to write English-like words","",""],["It always needs to be translated into object code or machine code","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per row. No mark if more than 1 tick for that row. Allow other indications of choice (e.g. cross) as long as clear."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","Answer"],"rows":[["The same language can be used on computers that use different hardware","High-level"],["It allows the user to directly manipulate memory","Low-level"],["It allows the user to write English-like words","High-level"],["It always needs to be translated into object code or machine code","High-level"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Examiner's Comments: The strongest responses showed a good understanding of the difference between low-level and high-level languages. Incorrect responses tended to be on the first row related to portability. A high-level language such as Python is portable, with translators available for many different types of processors. A low-level language is specific to one type of processor."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (same language used on different hardware): High-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (directly manipulate memory): Low-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (write English-like words): High-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (always needs to be translated into object code or machine code): High-level","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-026.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-5.png"}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"computational-thinking-techniques","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State the name of each of the following computational thinking techniques."},{"type":"text","value":"Breaking a complex problem down into smaller problems.\\n\\n____"},{"type":"text","value":"Hiding or removing irrelevant details from a problem to reduce the complexity.\\n\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Decomposition (for breaking a complex problem down into smaller problems). Abstraction (for hiding or removing irrelevant details). Correct answer only. Ignore spelling errors. Examiner's Comments: This question was answered well by the majority of candidates.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Decomposition","marks":1},{"criteria":"Abstraction","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-010.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-6.png"}},{"question_id":"7(a)","parent_question":7,"topic":"flowchart-trace","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program creates usernames for a school. The first design of the program is shown in the flowchart in Fig. 2."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/username-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Flowchart that builds a username from the first three letters of firstName and the first two letters of surname."},{"type":"text","value":"For example, using the process in Fig. 2, Tom Ward's username would be TomWa.\\n\\nState, using the process in Fig. 2, the username for Rebecca Ellis."}],"answer":{"guidance":"RebEl. Correct Answer Only (allow any case).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"RebEl (allow any case)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-011.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-a.png"}},{"question_id":"7(b)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"string-manipulation","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The program design is updated to create usernames as follows:\\n\\n- If the person is a teacher, their username is the last 3 letters of their surname and then the first 2 letters of their first name.\\n- If the person is a student, their username is the first 3 letters of their first name and then the first 2 letters of their surname.\\n\\nWhat would be the username for a teacher called Fred Biscuit using the updated process?"}],"answer":{"guidance":"uitFr. Correct Answer Only (allow any case). (Last 3 letters of 'Biscuit' = 'uit', first 2 letters of 'Fred' = 'Fr'.)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"uitFr (allow any case)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-011.png"],["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"7(b)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"algorithm-design-string","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm for the updated program design shown in (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example: Ask the user to input the data, store in variables firstname, surname and role. Check whether the role entered is teacher. If it is, join the right 3 most letters in surname with the left 2 letters in firstname. Store this in username. If it is not teacher, join the left 3 letters from firstname with the left 2 letters from surname. Store this in username. Output the value in username. 1 mark for each correct bullet to a maximum of 6. If used, a flowchart should represent the bulleted steps in the answer column.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Taking firstname, surname and teacher or student as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checking IF role is teacher/student (using appropriate selection)","marks":1},{"criteria":"For teacher: generating last 3 letters of surname using appropriate string manipulation","marks":1},{"criteria":"For teacher: generating first 2 letters of firstname and adding to previous","marks":1},{"criteria":"For student: correctly calculating (first 3 letters of firstname + first 2 letters of surname)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct concatenation and output","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"8(i)","parent_question":8,"topic":"abstraction-definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven.\\n\\nEach car is given a star rating of 1 to 5, based on the age of the car and the number of miles it has been driven. This rating is recorded in the computer system.\\n\\nDefine the term abstraction."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Hiding / ignoring / removing detail // focussing on certain parts of a problem.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Hiding / ignoring / removing detail, or focussing on certain parts of a problem","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-i.png"}},{"question_id":"8(ii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"abstraction-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one example of how abstraction has been used in the design of this star rating system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Focus on age / number of miles. Ignore other factors (such as make, model, etc). Allow other examples of factors to ignore / remove.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Focus on age / number of miles OR ignore other factors such as make, model, etc.","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"8(iii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"defensive-design-authentication","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how authentication could be used as part of the defensive design considerations for this computer system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ensures only certain users can access the system. Using password / other example of authentication technique. Allow other examples of authentication for BP2.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ensures only certain users can access the system","marks":1},{"criteria":"Using password / other example of authentication technique (e.g. biometric, PIN, 2FA)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-iii.png"}}]`),Z7={"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf"},e5={paper_id:U7,qualification:W7,component:V7,title:G7,year:Y7,session:K7,total_marks:X7,questions:Q7,maps:Z7},t5="J277-TOPIC-2.1.2",a5="J277",r5="TOPIC",i5="Designing, Creating and Refining Algorithms",n5=null,s5=null,o5=221,c5=JSON.parse('[{"question_id":"1","parent_question":1,"topic":"pin-validation-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"As part of a security system, the user is asked to choose a 4-digit value for their PIN. This PIN cannot be 1234 or 4321.\\n\\nCreate an algorithm that:\\n\\n- takes the PIN as input as a string\\n- checks that the PIN is 4 characters in length\\n- checks that the PIN is not either \\"1234\\" or \\"4321\\"\\n- outputs `\\"VALID PIN\\"` if the PIN meets all of the criteria\\n- outputs `\\"INVALID PIN\\"` if the PIN does not meet all of the criteria.\\n\\nYou do not need to check that the digits entered are numeric."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example code:"},{"type":"code","value":"pin = input(\\"Enter PIN\\")\\nif pin.length == 4 and pin != \\"1234\\" and pin != \\"4321\\" then\\n  print(\\"VALID PIN\\")\\nelse:\\n  print(\\"INVALID PIN\\")"},{"type":"list","items":["If diagram/flowchart given, ignore shapes if incorrect and mark text inside as algorithm.","Note - | or || means OR in some languages. AND is &.","BOD input and use/comparison of PIN as integer.","No need for quotation marks around 1234 / 4321.","Allow equivalent outputs.","BP3 and 4 can be combined or as separate checks. If combined, must logically work.","BP3 and 4 can be checks for positive or negative. Check must refer to variable for both comparisons.","BP5 given if invalid output (once or multiple times) for all invalid PINs even if valid also output.","BP6 only given if valid is output as the only output.","If sensible attempt at BP3 and BP4 (but incorrect), FT to BP5 and 6.","Section A so candidates can respond in pseudocode, structured English, flowcharts, etc. However, must not simply be a repeat of the question."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and storage/use of PIN","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at selection / condition controlled loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for 4 digits","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for both 1234 and 4321","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"INVALID PIN\\" after sensible attempt at all three checks","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"VALID PIN\\" after sensible attempt at all three checks, only when PIN is fully valid and no contradictory output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"ocr-reference-language-input","marks":1,"command_word":"Tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Rockfest is a yearly music festival. Visitors buy tickets to attend and watch the music acts.\\n\\nA computer system is created to sell tickets and store information about visitors and the music acts.\\n\\nVisitors are asked to choose a password. The password is entered twice and stored in the variables passA and passB.\\n\\nTick (✓) one box to identify the correct OCR Exam Reference Language statements for the design of this task."},{"type":"table","headers":["OCR Exam Reference Language to enter two passwords","Tick (✓) one box"],"rows":[["`passA and passB = input(\\"enter password twice\\")`",""],["`input(\\"enter your password\\") = passA`\\n`input(\\"enter your password again\\") = passB`",""],["`passA = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`passB = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""],["`\\"passA\\" = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`\\"passB\\" = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"3rd box ticked. The correct OCR Exam Reference Language uses the format: passA = input(\\"enter your password\\") and passB = input(\\"enter your password again\\").\\n\\nPleasingly, the vast majority of candidates were able to identify the correct syntax for code to store data in two distinct variables."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"3rd box ticked (passA = input(...); passB = input(...))","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"password-match-algorithm","marks":3,"command_word":"Design","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Design an algorithm that:\\n\\n- checks if the passwords stored in `passA` and `passB` are the same\\n- outputs `\\"passwords match\\"` if they are the same\\n- outputs `\\"passwords do not match\\"` if they are not the same."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example code:"},{"type":"code","value":"if passA == passB:\\n  print(\\"passwords match\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"passwords do not match\\")\\nend if"},{"type":"list","items":["Design so answer can be flowchart, etc. If flowchart, decision (diamond) box needed for BP1, True/False labels essential for BP2 and 3. Output (parallelogram) box needed for BP2 and 3.","Allow alternative messages. Ignore superfluous code (including inputs or overwriting variables) as long as it does not affect outcome. Could be done as separate if statements.","Do not allow elseif/elif etc by itself for BP1 unless complete logical comparison (e.g. elif passA != passB). FT to BP2 and 3.","BP2 and 3 require a sensible attempt at comparing variables in BP1 to be given. passA and passB must be correct with no spaces. Ignore case."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks if passA and passB are equal","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if True (\\"passwords match\\")","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if False (\\"passwords do not match\\")","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"function-definition-calculateprice","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tickets to the festival are £60 each. Visitors can buy multiple tickets. For example, a visitor buying 4 tickets would pay £240.\\n\\nWrite a function, `calculatePrice()` that:\\n\\n- takes the number of tickets to be purchased as a parameter\\n- calculates and returns the total price to pay.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\nfunction calculatePrice(num)\\n    price = num * 60\\n    return price\\nendfunction\\n```\\n\\nSection B so must be high level code. Do not accept x for multiplication. Do not accept spaces in function, variable or parameter names if clearly obvious.\\n\\nAll code must be within function definition (if present). BP3 – if no parameter present, allow multiplying by sensible value (e.g. input value). FT overwriting parameter.\\n\\nBP4 must be return. Do not credit output/print. Value returned can be with or without £ sign and other text. FT for BP4 if sensible attempt made at calculation.\\n\\nAccept multiple parameters passed in. Accept returning by assigning to function name (e.g. calculatePrice = price), some languages such as VB do this.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function definition for calculatePrice()","marks":1},{"criteria":"...with parameter that is not overwritten","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculate price based on multiplying parameter (if present) by 60","marks":1},{"criteria":"...always return calculated price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"function-call-calculateprice","marks":2,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:\\n\\n- uses the function `calculatePrice()` to find the price for a visitor buying 3 tickets\\n- stores the result in the variable `totalPrice`\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\ntotalPrice = calculatePrice(3)\\n```\\n\\nDo not accept == for assignment. Allow FT to BP2 only if sensible attempt at BP1 (e.g. missing/wrong parameter).\\n\\nAllow multiple values passed in as long as 3 is one of the values. Do not accept definitions of a function – must be calling the existing function. Penalise spaces in variable / function name only if clearly obvious. Ignore superfluous code. Ignore case.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"calculatePrice(3) called","marks":1},{"criteria":"...return value assigned to totalPrice","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"trace-table-for-loop","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The number of different music acts on each of the three stages is stored in the array `actNumbers`.\\n\\nEach index of the array represents one stage."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2"],"rows":[["Data","10","14","6"]]},{"type":"text","value":"`actNumbers`"},{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm adds up the total number of acts on all three stages."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 count = 0\\n02 for x = 0 to 2\\n03 count = count + actNumbers[x]\\n04 next x\\n05 print(count)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm. You may not need to use all rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","count","x","Output"],"rows":[["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore line numbers - not required. Mark order of values. Values can be repeated in count and x columns, look at changes.\\n\\nFT for BP4 if incorrect answer calculated but output. Allow last value of count calculated to be output for the mark. Give BOD for \\"-\\", \\"nothing\\", etc as extra in output column.\\n\\nTrace: count initially 0; count increases to 10, 24, 30; x goes from 0, 1, 2 (, 3); 30 output on final line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Count initially set to 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"count increases to 10, 24, 30 and no other changes","marks":1},{"criteria":"x goes from 0, 1, 2 (, 3) and no other changes","marks":1},{"criteria":"30 output and no other output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"refining-loop-for-new-array","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The array `actNumbers` is changed to include data for two more stages. The new array is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4"],"rows":[["Data","10","14","6","9","11"]]},{"type":"text","value":"`actNumbers`"},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm from part (i) needs refining to work for the new array. The algorithm is repeated here:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 count = 0\\n02 for x = 0 to 2\\n03 count = count + actNumbers[x]\\n04 next x\\n05 print(count)"},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the line number that will need to be changed for the new array and write the updated line of code."},{"type":"text","value":"Line number ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Updated code ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must use x as counter variable. Accept rewrites in other languages.\\n\\nBOD 0 to 5 / range(5) etc. Accept other changes that implement the change successfully e.g. using length of actNumbers.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 02 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"For x = 0 to 4 (updated code)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(iii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"refining-output-concatenation","marks":1,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Refine line 05 in the algorithm to output `\\"The total act count is \\"` and then the content of the variable `count`."},{"type":"text","value":"For example:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"The total act count is 50"},{"type":"text","value":"New line 05 ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nprint(\\"The total act count is \\" + count)\\n```\\n\\nAllow other concatenation operators e.g. & (VB), dot (PHP, Rust), double dot (Lua), etc. Allow comma. Must have some form of separator. Ignore spacing. Ignore case. Ignore casting.\\n\\nAllow other commands that are equivalent to print (e.g. echo, output, etc). Allow multiple print statements. Allow string interpolation or language specific examples e.g. f strings in Python, $ before variables in PHP.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"print(\\"The total act count is \\" + count) or equivalent with separator","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(d)","parent_question":2,"topic":"ticket-booking-loop","marks":6,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 500 tickets available for the music festival. The number of tickets currently available is stored in the variable `tickets`.\\n\\nComplete the algorithm to:\\n\\n- prompt the user to enter how many tickets they would like and take this value as input\\n- output `\\"Tickets booked\\"` and reduce the value stored in `tickets` if there are enough tickets available\\n- output `\\"Not enough tickets\\"` if there are not enough tickets available\\n- repeat the steps until there are no tickets left.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"tickets = 500"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\ntickets = 500\\nwhile tickets > 0\\n  num = input(\\"enter num tickets\\")\\n  if tickets >= num then\\n    print(\\"tickets booked\\")\\n    tickets = tickets - num\\n  else\\n    print(\\"not enough tickets\\")\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\n```\\n\\nAllow any suitable confirmation message for BP3 (booked / not enough) but must be right way around. BP2 must allow tickets to be booked when exact number remain.\\n\\nBP3 and 4 dependent on BP2; if BP2 incorrect / not given but reasonable attempt (e.g. checking if tickets bigger than 0) allow FT for BP3/4. Any attempt at loop means giving BP5.\\n\\nBP6 must not repeatedly reset tickets to 500. BP6 must include repeated input (if present).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"input and store/use number of tickets needed","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if enough tickets left","marks":1},{"criteria":"sensible confirmation message output correctly for both booked and no tickets available","marks":1},{"criteria":"tickets variable updated correctly","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"repeat all above while tickets > 0 / != 0 / until <= 0","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"range-validation-if","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program asks a user to enter a number. Only integer values between 1 and 100 (inclusive) are accepted. The program is tested.\\n\\nAn algorithm is written for the program.\\n\\nComplete the algorithm for the program."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"num = input(\\"Enter a number between 1 and 100\\")\\nif num ____ AND ____ then\\n  print(\\"accepted\\")\\n____\\n  print(\\"not accepted\\")\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow check either way around (e.g. could check higher boundary first) for BP1 and BP2. BP2 must include reference to num variable.\\n\\nBP3 Do not allow elseif / elif unless full correct logical comparison included (e.g. elseif num < 1 or num > 100). Section A so allow \\"less than or equal to\\" etc.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":">= 1 // > 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"num <= 100 // num < 101","marks":1},{"criteria":"else","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"identify-variable","marks":1,"command_word":"Give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the identifier of one variable used in the algorithm in part (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"num - the only variable identifier used in the algorithm. Do not allow any other code around variable identifier. Ignore capitalisation. Ignore minor spelling errors.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"num","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(iii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"identify-boolean-operator","marks":1,"command_word":"Give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one Boolean operator used in the algorithm in part (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"AND - the Boolean operator joining the two comparisons. Do not allow any other code around operator. Ignore capitalisation. Ignore minor spelling errors.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"AND","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"flowchart-iteration-trace","marks":2,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This flowchart uses iteration.\\n\\nComplete the table by identifying how many times \\"OK\\" will be output for when each value is input."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/iteration-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Iteration flowchart that repeatedly outputs OK and subtracts 2 from x until x is less than 0."},{"type":"table","headers":["Input value","Number of times \\"OK\\" is output"],"rows":[["3",""],["10",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"This question involved following a flowchart to determine the outcome produced. For input value of 3, OK is output 2 times. For input value of 10, OK is output 6 times.\\n\\nMost students successfully determined this for input 3 but fewer for input 10, due to confusion over the use of the \'less than\' operator when x was set to zero.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"2 (for input 3)","marks":1},{"criteria":"6 (for input 10)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"data-types","marks":3,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Students take part in a sports day. The students are put into teams.\\n\\nStudents gain points depending on their result and the year group they are in. The points are added to the team score.\\n\\nThe team with the most points at the end of the sports day wins.\\n\\nData about the teams and students is stored in a sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify the most appropriate data type for each variable used by the program.\\n\\nEach data type must be different."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Example","Data type"],"rows":[["`teamName`","\\"Super-Team\\"",""],["`studentYearGroup`","11",""],["`javelinThrow`","18.2",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept alternative equivalent correct data types (e.g. single/double/decimal for BP3). Do not accept char for BP1.\\n\\nThis question was completed very well by the vast majority of candidates, showing that data types are now well understood.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"teamName: String","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentYearGroup: Integer","marks":1},{"criteria":"javelinThrow: Real / Float","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"linear-search-function","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student names for a team are stored in an array with the identifier `theTeam`. An example of the data in this array is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Data","Ali","Eve","Ling","Nina","Sarah","Tom"]]},{"type":"text","value":"A linear search function is used to find whether a student is in the team. The function:\\n- takes a student name as a parameter\\n- returns True if the student name is in the array\\n- returns False if the student name is not in the array.\\n\\nComplete the design of an algorithm for the linear search function."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"function linearSearch(studentName)\\n  for count = 0 to ____\\n    if theTeam[____] == ____ then\\n      return ____\\n    endif\\n  next count\\n  return False\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept 6 // theTeam.length() for BP1 (Python). Accept alternative length functions e.g. len(). Accept count = 5 (and equivalents) for BP1.\\n\\nAccept \\"True\\" for BP4. Do not allow obvious spaces in variable names. Ignore capitalisation.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"theTeam.length() - 1 // 5","marks":1},{"criteria":"count (as index)","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentName","marks":1},{"criteria":"True","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"trace-table-javelin-score","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm calculates the number of points a student gets for the distance they throw in the javelin:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 javelinThrow = input(\\"Enter distance\\")\\n02 yearGroup = input(\\"Enter year group\\")\\n03 if javelinThrow >= 20.0 then\\n04 score = 3\\n05 elseif javelinThrow >= 10.0 then\\n06 score = 2\\n07 else\\n08 score = 1\\n09 endif\\n10 if yearGroup != 11 then\\n11 score = score * 2\\n12 endif\\n13 print(\\"The score is\\", score)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm when a student in year 10 throws a distance of 14.3. You may not need to use all the rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","javelinThrow","yearGroup","score","Output"],"rows":[["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if in wrong order or additional (incorrect) changes. Penalise line numbers once then FT.\\n\\nAllow FT for BP4 for current value of score. BP4 must not include comma. Ignore superfluous spaces. Ignore quotation marks. Treat any entry in output column as an output, even if \\"x\\", \\"-\\" or \\"0\\".\\n\\nAnswer may include lines where no changes or output happens (i.e. lines 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12). Where variable doesn\'t change, current value may be repeated on subsequent lines.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"javelinThrow set to 14.3 on line 01 and yearGroup set to 10 on line 02","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 2 on line 06","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 4 on line 11","marks":1},{"criteria":"\\"The score is 4\\" output on line 13 with no additional outputs (allow input statements)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"height-validation-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated.\\n\\nThe height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \\"VALID\\" or \\"NOT VALID\\" depending on the height input.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (checking for valid input):\\n\\n```\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nExample 2 (checking for invalid input):\\n\\n```\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nAnswers using AND/OR for BP2 and BP3 must be logically correct. Do not accept if height >=40 and <=180. Answers using OR will reverse output for BP4. BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3. Ignore conversion to int on input. input cannot be used as a variable name. Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for a high-level language / ERL.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 // < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 // > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"test-data-plan","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests.\\n\\nComplete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow FT for BP4 if reasonable attempt at validating (must include at least one boundary). No need to include decimals, e.g. accept 50. Ignore cm if given.\\n\\nAnswer must be actual data (e.g. 50) and not description of data. If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for BP3.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: Any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"sql-where-clause","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database.\\n\\nThe database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n________________"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1.\\n\\nSpelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"abstraction-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design) that is applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is.\\n\\nExamples: \\"focus on student names and events\\"; \\"ignore data such as students\' favourite subjects\\"; \\"store year groups instead of ages or DOB\\"; \\"shows student IDs instead of full student details\\".","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data in context of sports day","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"decomposition-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables. Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description.\\n\\nExamples: \\"splits the program up into different events\\"; \\"separates the validation routines into subroutines\\"; \\"breaks the database down into a table per event\\". Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking the program into sections/subroutines OR database into tables (in context)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(f)","parent_question":5,"topic":"highest-score-iteration","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score).\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example 1:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"highscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)"},{"type":"list","items":["For BP3, allow \\"stop\\" for either team name or score (or both).","Allow use of break to exit loop.","Allow recursive function(s) for BP2 and BP3.","Initialisation of variables is not needed.","Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score; assume only one team has the highest score.","BP4 and BP5 could be done in many ways. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists.","FT for BP6 if an attempt is made at calculating highest score/name.","If an answer simply asks for multiple entries without iteration, BP2 and BP3 cannot be accessed, but all other marks remain available.","`input` cannot be used as a variable name."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store/use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"...to enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"flowchart-odd-even","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","response_style":"annotate-image","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm decides if a number is odd or even.\\n\\nAn odd number divided by 2 will give the remainder 1.\\n\\nThe flowchart statements have been written for the algorithm, but the flowchart is incomplete.\\n\\nComplete the flowchart."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/topics/ocr/2.1.2_q6_odd_even_flowchart.png","alt_text":"Incomplete flowchart for the odd or even number algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"No need for arrows – lines are acceptable. BOD for correct answers that include a loop back to the start.\\n\\nThe majority of candidates recognised the correct flowchart shapes for a decision and many used the parallelogram shape for inputs and outputs. Fewer correctly connected the boxes so all paths started and ended at appropriate points.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct shape for all three inputs AND outputs (parallelogram)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct shape for decision (diamond)","marks":1},{"criteria":"True and False // Yes and No labelled correctly (True/Yes linking to \\"Even\\")","marks":1},{"criteria":"All lines joined up correctly and link to End","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_question":7,"topic":"identify-inputs-outputs","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program allows users to search for and watch videos. Users give a rating to the videos they watch.\\n\\nIdentify one input and one output for the program."},{"type":"text","value":"Input ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Output ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a suitable input, 1 mark for a suitable output. Allow input / print pseudocode statements if meets mark point(s). Does not have to be valid pseudocode. Do not allow examples of inputs (e.g. \\"music videos\\").\\n\\nInput e.g.: Name/keyword for video (to be searched for) // search text; Controls for watching video (e.g. play/pause); Rating given to video.\\n\\nOutput e.g.: Video to be watched // audio; Results of search; (total/overall/average) rating of video; Number of views (of video); Confirmation of data entry / data validity; Messages to user (e.g. \\"enter a rating\\", \\"your rating has been saved\\") in quotation marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Suitable input (e.g. search keyword, controls, rating)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Suitable output (e.g. video/audio, search results, rating, message)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8","parent_question":8,"topic":"refine-search-count","marks":4,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An array stores a collection of words. The array has the identifier `data`."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"data = [\\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\", \\"when\\", \\"and\\", \\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"A searching algorithm is used to find a word in the array `data`. The algorithm used to search for a word is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"word = input(\\"Enter a word to find\\")\\nfound = False\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == word then\\n    found = True\\n  endif\\nnext i\\nprint(found)"},{"type":"text","value":"Some words appear in the array more than once.\\n\\nRefine the algorithm to:\\n- count the number of times a word appears in the array\\n- output this number.\\n\\nFor example, the algorithm should output 3 if the input is \\"and\\"."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example answer:\\n\\n```\\nsearch = input(\\"Enter a word\\")\\ncount = 0\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == search then\\n    count = count + 1\\nnext i\\nprint(count)\\n```\\n\\nAllow answers that completely rewrite the algorithm as long as points on left met.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Declares variable to store the count (before the for loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Initialises variable to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"Increments variable by 1 each time the word is found","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs count value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(a)(i)","parent_question":9,"topic":"sql-select-where-and","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Drones flies goods around the country using drones.\\n\\nDetails about the drones that pilots fly are stored in a database table called TblDrone. Some of the data stored in this table is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["DroneID","DroneType","Mileage","LastCheck"],"rows":[["001","Quadcopter","65 032","65 000"],["002","Quadcopter","32 128","21 000"],["003","Octocopter","98 021","98 000"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to display DroneID and Mileage for all Octocopter type drones that have a mileage of greater than 50 000 miles."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT ____\\n____ TblDrone\\nWHERE DroneType = \\"Octocopter\\" ____ Mileage ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept SELECT * or selection of additional fields for BP1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"DroneID, Mileage","marks":1},{"criteria":"FROM","marks":1},{"criteria":"AND","marks":1},{"criteria":"> 50000","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(a)(ii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"flowchart-drone-check","marks":2,"command_word":"Complete","response_style":"annotate-image","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Drones must be checked every 10 000 miles. If the difference between Mileage and LastCheck is greater than 10 000 then the drone needs to be checked.\\n\\nA flowchart shows the steps needed to check a drone.\\n\\nThe flowchart outputs \\"Check\\" if the drone needs to be checked.\\nThe flowchart outputs \\"No Check\\" if the drone does not need to be checked.\\n\\nComplete the flowchart for the algorithm."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/topics/ocr/2.1.2_q9_drone_check_flowchart.png","alt_text":"Incomplete flowchart for deciding whether a drone needs to be checked."}],"answer":{"guidance":"BP1 could be >, < or either in words. Ignore case and minor misspellings.\\n\\nBP2 (output) could be either way around depending on comparison for BP1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"if Mileage - LastCheck > 10000","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output \\"Check\\" and \\"No Check\\" or equivalent correctly based on logical check for BP1","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(b)","parent_question":9,"topic":"trace-table-while-loop","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pilot code is automatically generated when a new pilot joins the company.\\n\\nThis algorithm generates a code for each pilot:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 a = input(\\"Enter first letter of first name\\")\\n02 b = input(\\"Enter first letter of second name\\")\\n03 c = random(1,100)\\n04 while c < 100\\n05 c = c * 10\\n06 endwhile\\n07 pilotCode = a + b + str(c)\\n08 print(pilotCode)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the given algorithm.\\n\\nLines 01 to 03 have already been completed.\\n\\nYou may not need to use all rows in the trace table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","a","b","c","pilotCode","Output"],"rows":[["01","H","","","",""],["02","","K","","",""],["03","","","9","",""],["","","","","",""],["","","","","",""],["","","","","",""],["","","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore additional lines that do not affect outcome. FT for missing or incorrect line numbers. FT for output based on incorrect tracing of loop.\\n\\nExpected trace (continuing from lines 01-03):\\nLine 05: c = 90\\nLine 05: c = 900\\nLine 07: pilotCode = HK900\\nLine 08: HK900 output","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"c = 90 on line 05","marks":1},{"criteria":"c = 900 on line 05","marks":1},{"criteria":"pilotCode = HK900 on line 07","marks":1},{"criteria":"HK900 output on line 08","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(a)","parent_question":10,"topic":"pseudocode-sum","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The variables num1 and num2 store integers.\\n\\nWrite pseudocode to add the integers stored in num1 and num2. Store the result in a variable with the identifier total."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other logically valid responses that result in total storing the correct value. Accept other suitable assignment operators.\\n\\nExamples: total = sum(num1, num2); total = num2 + num1; x = num1 + num2 then total = x. Ignore any values given to the variable. Ignore capitalisation and minor misspelling.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"total = num1 + num2 (or logically valid equivalent)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(i)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator-power","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Three incomplete pseudocode algorithms are given with a description of the purpose of each algorithm.\\n\\nWrite the missing arithmetic operator for each algorithm.\\n\\nOutputting 12 to the power of 2."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"print(12 ____ 2)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept ** or other sensible operator that indicates raising to a power. If pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. pow), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"^ (or ** / pow etc)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(ii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Working out if a number is odd or even."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"number = 53\\nif number ____ 2 == 0 then\\n  print(\\"Even number\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"Odd number\\")\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept % or other sensible operator that indicates modulus. If pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. modulo), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"MOD (or % / modulo)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(iii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator-subtraction","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding the difference between two measurements."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"measurement1 = 300\\nmeasurement2 = 100\\ndifference = measurement1 ____ measurement2"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept other sensible operator that indicates subtraction. If a pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. minus), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"- (minus)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(c)","parent_question":10,"topic":"trace-table-do-until","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Read the following pseudocode algorithm:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 start = 3\\n02 do\\n03 print(start)\\n04 start = start - 1\\n05 until start == -1\\n06 print(\\"Finished\\")"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following trace table for the given algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","start","Output"],"rows":[["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore lines 02 and 05 in answer unless these change or output any values. Candidate may repeat start value when unchanged, this is acceptable.\\n\\nPenalise incorrect or missing line numbers or additional output once only then FT. -1 must not be output for BP2. Penalise missing or incorrect output once only for BP1 and FT for missing or incorrect output for BP2.\\n\\nFinished may be with or without quotes. Ignore case or minor spelling error. Max 2 marks if any incorrect output or changes to start. Do not accept calculated values of start (e.g. 3-1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Start is set to 3 on line 01 and 3 is output on line 03","marks":1},{"criteria":"2, 1 and 0 are output on next 3 iterations with start updated to 2, 1, 0, -1 on correct line numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Finished is output on line 06","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11","parent_question":11,"topic":"increment-variable","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write pseudocode to increment a value held in a variable score by one."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other logically correct answers that result in score increasing by one and being overwritten. Do not accept score + 1 / score = +1.\\n\\nAccept valid structured English answers that refer to score increasing and overwriting the existing value by one. e.g. \\"score becomes/equals score plus one\\". Ignore any superfluous code that does not affect the outcome.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"score = score + 1 // score += 1 // score++","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12","parent_question":12,"topic":"total-and-average-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Jack is writing a program to add up some numbers. His first attempt at the program is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"a = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nb = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nc = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nd = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\ne = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nf = (a + b + c + d + e)\\nprint(f)"},{"type":"text","value":"Jack decides to improve his program. He wants to be able to input how many numbers to add together each time the algorithm runs, and also wants it to calculate and display the average of these numbers.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- ask the user to input the quantity of numbers they want to enter and read this value as input\\n- repeatedly take a number as input, until the quantity of numbers the user input has been entered\\n- calculate and output the total of these numbers\\n- calculate and output the average of these numbers."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nnum = input(\\"Enter how many numbers\\")\\nfor x = 1 to num\\n  temp = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\n  total = total + temp\\nnext x\\nprint(total)\\nprint(total / num)\\n```\\n\\nIf flow chart used, correct shapes needed. Allow tolerance of 1 with number of loops for BP3 with for loops. BP1 requires input with a message (can be two statements, e.g. print and then input or combined). Input must be stored or used.\\n\\nBP3, 4, 5 must be logically correct to be credited. Ignore non-initialisation of total. BP5 can be given as FT as long as an attempt has been made at working out a total within the loop. BP6 can be given as FT long as attempt made at total and average (not necessarily in a loop).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"input and stores/uses value with message","marks":1},{"criteria":"attempt at repeating","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly repeats number of times given as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly take number as input within loop and calculates total of these numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly calculate an average (total/num)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output both total and average","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(a)(i)","parent_question":13,"topic":"hotel-booking-validation","marks":5,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Customers at a hotel can stay between 1 and 5 (inclusive) nights and can choose between a basic room or a premium room.\\n\\nWhen a new booking is recorded, the details are entered into a program to validate the values. The following criteria are checked:\\n- firstName and surname are not empty\\n- room is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"\\n- nights is between 1 and 5 (inclusive).\\n\\nIf any invalid data is found \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\nIf all data is valid \\"ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\n\\nComplete the following program to validate the inputs.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"firstName = input(\\"Enter a first name\\")\\nsurname = input(\\"Enter a surname\\")\\nroom = input(\\"Enter basic or premium\\")\\nnights = input(\\"Enter between 1 and 5 nights\\")\\nstayComplete = False"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nvalid = True\\nif firstname == \\"\\" or surname == \\"\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nend if\\nif room != \\"basic\\" and room != \\"premium\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif nights < 1 or nights > 5 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif valid then\\n  print(\\"ALLOWED\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"NOT ALLOWED\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nMust have some attempt at all three checks to give output mark(s). Check for nights must check both upper and lower limits. Iteration can be used as validation if input repeatedly asked for until valid answer given.\\n\\nDo not accept logically incorrect Boolean conditions such as \'if firstname or surname == \\"\\"\'. BP1 to 3 can check for valid or invalid inputs. Pay particular attention to use of AND / OR. Only give marks for output if these work together correctly.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks that both firstname and surname are not empty","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that room is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that nights is between 1 and 5 (inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) if any of the 3 checks are invalid","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) only if all three checks are valid","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(a)(ii)","parent_question":13,"topic":"test-plan-nights","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan to check whether the number of nights is validated correctly."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (number of nights)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["2","","ALLOWED"],["","Boundary","ALLOWED"],["","Erroneous / Invalid","NOT ALLOWED"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other descriptions that mean normal (e.g. valid / typical / acceptable).\\n\\nTest data 2: Normal, ALLOWED.\\nTest data 1 // 5: Boundary, ALLOWED (not 0 or 6 as says allowed).\\nTest data e.g. 7: Erroneous/Invalid, NOT ALLOWED (any numeric value except 1 to 5 // any non-numeric input e.g. \\"bananas\\").","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal (type for test data 2)","marks":1},{"criteria":"1 or 5 (boundary test data, not 0 or 6)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Any numeric value except 1 to 5 // non-numeric (erroneous test data)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(b)(i)","parent_question":13,"topic":"newprice-function","marks":4,"command_word":"Create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A Basic room costs £60 each night. A Premium room costs £80 each night.\\n\\nCreate a function, `newPrice()`, that takes the number of nights and the type of room as parameters, calculates and returns the price to pay.\\n\\nYou do not have to validate these parameters.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nfunction newPrice(nights, room)\\n  if room == \\"basic\\" then\\n    price = 60 * nights\\n  elseif room == \\"premium\\" then\\n    price = 80 * nights\\n  endif\\n  return price\\nendfunction\\n```\\n\\nBP1 must be clear that a new function is being defined. E.g. function / def keyword. Allow FT for subsequent marks if not present. Ignore any code outside attempt at function definition. Ignore additional parameters. Ignore inputs or additional code as long as these do not overwrite parameters. If inputs used instead of parameters, FT for BP3.\\n\\nAllow use of else for second room type in BP3. Must return (not output) value. Return can be done e.g. in VB by assigning to function name (e.g. newPrice = price).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function header for newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"...taking (at least) two parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates price based on parameters within function","marks":1},{"criteria":"...returns this calculated price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(b)(ii)","parent_question":13,"topic":"newprice-function-call","marks":3,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write program code, that uses `newPrice()`, to output the price of staying in a Premium room for 5 nights.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nprint(newPrice(\\"premium\\", 5))\\n```\\n\\nor\\n\\n```\\nx = newPrice(5, \\"premium\\")\\nprint(x)\\n```\\n\\nOrder of parameters not important. \\"premium\\" must use string delimiters (e.g. quotes). Do not allow function definitions for BP1. Ignore capitalisation of newPrice.\\n\\nCandidate could store returned value in a variable and then print this, or store parameters in variables before passing in. Ignore any superfluous code given. Do not credit answers where newPrice is overwritten prior to use. Ignore spaces. Allow function call if brackets missing.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Call function newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"...with (\\"premium\\", 5) as parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output returned value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(c)","parent_question":13,"topic":"refine-logic-errors","marks":2,"command_word":"Describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hotel has nine rooms that are numbered from room 0 to room 8.\\n\\nThe number of people currently staying in each room is stored in an array with the identifier room.\\n\\nThe index of room represents the room number."},{"type":"text","value":"Array `room`"},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5","6","7","8"],"rows":[["Data","2","1","3","2","1","0","0","4","1"]]},{"type":"text","value":"The following program counts how many people are currently staying in the hotel."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"for count = 1 to 8\\n  total = 0\\n  total = total + room[count]\\nnext count\\nprint(total)"},{"type":"text","value":"When tested, the program is found to contain two logic errors.\\n\\nDescribe how the program can be refined to remove these logic errors."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow loop changed to 0 to 8 or 0 to 9 (Python). Do not accept moving total outside loop if moved to after loop (would still be a logic error). Do not accept move to top of loop.\\n\\nAccept corrected code shown. Accept reference to count variable limits for BP1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"For loop changed to include 0 (e.g. 0 to 8)","marks":1},{"criteria":"total = 0 moved to before loop starts / removed","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(d)","parent_question":13,"topic":"car-park-pricing-loop","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hotel car park charges £4 per hour. If the car is electric, this price is halved to £2 per hour.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- take as input the number of hours the user has parked and whether their car is electric or not\\n- calculate and output the total price\\n- repeat continually until the user enters 0 hours.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nwhile hours != 0\\n  hours = input(\\"Enter hours\\")\\n  electric = input(\\"enter Y for electric or N\\")\\n  if electric == \\"Y\\" then\\n    price = hours * 2\\n  elseif electric == \\"N\\" then\\n    price = hours * 4\\n  endif\\n  print(price)\\nendwhile\\n```\\n\\nInitialisation of price and hours not necessary, but if present hours must be non-zero for BP6 to be given. BP5 must include all points attempted.\\n\\nBP6 can be given as FT even if BP5 (loop) is in the wrong place. BP6 could be achieved as repeated function calls / recursion.\\n\\nInitial input outside of loop that is then also included within loop is fine. Do not accept while hours > 0 (could be -1). Do not penalise answers where 0 is output when loop exits.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs hours AND electric (two separate inputs), storing or using these","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if car is electric (IF/Select statement)","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 2 // price / 2) for electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 4 // electric price * 2) for non-electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at repetition of above","marks":1},{"criteria":"...until 0 hours entered","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(a)","parent_question":14,"topic":"truth-table-nand","marks":2,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the truth table in Fig. 1 for the Boolean statement P = NOT(A AND B)."},{"type":"table","headers":["A","B","P"],"rows":[["0","0","1"],["0","1","____"],["1","0","____"],["1","1","0"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct answer in table. \'True\' or \'T\' are also credit worthy.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"P = 1 for A=0, B=1","marks":1},{"criteria":"P = 1 for A=1, B=0","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(b)","parent_question":14,"topic":"logic-diagram-nand","marks":1,"command_word":"Tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box to identify the correct logic diagram for P = NOT(A AND B).\\n\\nP = NOT(A AND B)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Logic diagram","Tick one box"],"rows":[[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/a.png","alt_text":"Option a logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/b.png","alt_text":"Option b logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/c.png","alt_text":"Option c logic-gate diagram."},""]]}],"answer":{"auto_mark":{"type":"single_tick_table","correct_key":"1:1"},"guidance":"Correct Answer Only. The correct logic diagram is the NAND gate equivalent (AND followed by NOT).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct logic diagram identified (NAND / AND-NOT)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(a)","parent_question":15,"topic":"pseudocode-max-of-two","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n- Input two numbers from the user\\n- Compare both numbers and output the largest number.\\n\\nComplete the pseudocode for this program."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"num1 = input(\\"enter first number\\")\\nnum2 = input(\\"enter second number\\")\\n____ num1 > ____ then\\n  ____\\nelse\\n  ____\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow equivalent pseudocode expressions. Variables must not have speech marks around them. Correct completed code is:\\n\\n```\\nif num1 > num2 then\\n  print(num1)\\nelse\\n  print(num2)\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nEach blank is position-locked and must be marked against its intended position only: blank 1 (before `num1 >`) must be `if`; blank 2 (after `num1 >`) must be `num2`; blank 3 (inside the if branch) must be `print(num1)`; blank 4 (inside the else branch) must be `print(num2)`. Do not award a mark for a correct item placed in the wrong blank.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Blank 1 (before `num1 >`): if","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 2 (after `num1 >`): num2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 3 (if branch): print(num1)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 4 (else branch): print(num2)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(b)","parent_question":15,"topic":"doubling-loop-algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A second program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n- Input a number from the user\\n- Double the number input and print the result\\n- Repeat bullets 1 and 2 until the user enters a number less than 0.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm for this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (structured English):\\n\\n```\\nstore 10 in number\\nwhile number is greater than or equal to 0\\ndo the following:\\n  Take input from the user, store in number\\n  Multiply number by 2\\n  Output number\\n```\\n\\nExample 2 (pseudocode):\\n\\n```\\nwhile number >= 0\\n  number = input()\\n  output(number * 2)\\n```\\n\\nIgnore non-initialisation of value used in condition for loop.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"use of condition controlled loop (while or do/until)","marks":1},{"criteria":"checking condition of number larger than or equal to 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Input number from user within loop (FT if no loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"multiply number input by 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"output value in number","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"16","parent_question":16,"topic":"complex-boolean-cinema","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A cinema uses the following criteria to decide if a customer is allowed to see a film that has a 15 rating:\\n\\nCustomers have to be 15 years of age or older to see the film. They also need to either have a ticket or have the money to buy a ticket.\\n\\nThe table shows the inputs to the system that will output whether the customer can watch the film."},{"type":"table","headers":["Input","Criteria (True / False)"],"rows":[["A","The customer is 15 or over"],["B","The customer has a ticket"],["C","The customer has the money to buy a ticket"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following algorithm to output whether the customer is allowed to see the film or not."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"A = input(\\"Is the customer 15 or over?\\")\\nB = input(\\"Does the customer have a ticket?\\")\\nC = input(\\"Does the customer have money to buy a ticket?\\")"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nif A AND (B OR C) then\\n  print(\\"allowed\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not allowed\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nAccept answers where inputs are given as strings e.g.:\\nif A == \\"Yes\\" AND (B == \\"Yes\\" OR C == \\"Yes\\") then...","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Logically compares A AND // correct nested IF","marks":1},{"criteria":"...B OR C // correct sequential IF","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output in both cases (with attempt at selection)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(i)","parent_question":17,"topic":"car-validation-algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven.\\n\\nThe car dealership only sells cars that have fewer than 10 000 miles and are 5 years old or less.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm that will:\\n- ask the user to enter the number of miles and the age of a car\\n- validate the input to check that only sensible values that are in the given range are entered\\n- output True if valid data has been entered or False if invalid data has been entered."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nmiles = input(\\"enter miles driven\\")\\nage = input(\\"enter age of car\\")\\nvalid = True\\nif miles > 10000 or miles < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nelseif age > 5 or age < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nprint(valid)\\n```\\n\\nBP2 and 3 must check for both ends of range – must check that input data is not negative. Allow FT for BP4 if already penalised under BP2 and/or 3 and output is otherwise correct.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Miles and age input separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid mileage","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid age","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks both are greater than / greater than equal to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly outputs both True and False","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(ii)","parent_question":17,"topic":"car-validation-test-data","marks":3,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The validation routine from part (i) must be tested with normal, erroneous and boundary test data.\\n\\nIdentify suitable test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Miles","Age"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Erroneous","",""],["Boundary","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Specific data must be given, not a description.\\n\\nNormal: miles (0 – 9,999), age (0 - 5). e.g. Normal 7,000 / 3.\\nErroneous: miles (less than 0, larger than 9,999), age (less than 0 / more than 5) // non-numeric data. e.g. Erroneous 12,000 / 7.\\nBoundary: miles (-1/0 / 9,999 / 10,000), age (-1/0 / 5/6). e.g. Boundary 10,000 / 5.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: miles 0-9999, age 0-5","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: miles out of range, age out of range or non-numeric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: miles -1/0/9999/10000, age -1/0/5/6","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(iii)","parent_question":17,"topic":"iterative-testing","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify when iterative testing is performed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Iterative testing is performed during the development process while writing the program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"During development // whilst writing the program // before development is complete","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(b)","parent_question":17,"topic":"battery-charge-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership sells electric cars, which require charging before they can be driven. Charging the battery by 1% takes 10 minutes.\\n\\nFor example, a battery has 80% charge. It would take 200 minutes, or 3 hours and 20 minutes to charge to 100%.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm that:\\n- asks the user for the current battery charge percentage\\n- outputs \\"full\\" for a battery currently at 100%\\n- calculates how long this battery would take to charge\\n- outputs this in hours and minutes."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\ncharge = input(\\"enter battery charge\\")\\nif charge == 100 then\\n  print(\\"full\\")\\nelse\\n  time = (100-charge) * 10\\n  hours = time DIV 60\\n  mins = time MOD 60\\n  print(hours, mins)\\nendif\\n\\nAllow output of 0 hours 0 minutes if full. Allow answers referencing decimal parts (e.g. 0.8 = 80%). BP5 can be attempted in many ways (e.g. DIV and MOD, repeated division, etc). Allow FT for BP6 if reasonable attempt at conversion for BP5 has been given.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs the current battery charge percentage","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"full\\" if 100%","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the amount to charge","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the time in minutes","marks":1},{"criteria":"converts to hours and minutes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs the time in hours and minutes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(a)(i)","parent_question":18,"topic":"sale-price-test-plan","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Tech is an online shop that sells electronics such as TVs and game consoles.\\n\\nThe following flowchart shows an algorithm to calculate the price of an item during a sale period."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/sale-price-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Sale-price flowchart that reduces prices over 200 by 40 and prices over 100 by 20 before output."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Price input","Test type","Expected price output"],"rows":[["50","Normal",""],["100","Boundary",""],["150","Normal",""],["200","Boundary",""],["250","Normal",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark if two correct, two marks if four correct, three marks if all correct.\\n\\nExpected outputs:\\n50 Normal -> 50\\n100 Boundary -> 100\\n150 Normal -> 130\\n200 Boundary -> 180\\n250 Normal -> 210","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Two correct outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"Four correct outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"All five correct outputs (50, 100, 130, 180, 210)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(a)(ii)","parent_question":18,"topic":"sale-price-rewrite","marks":6,"command_word":"Rewrite","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewrite the algorithm above.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nprice = input(\\"enter price\\")\\nif price > 200 then\\n  price = price - 40\\nelseif price > 100 then\\n  price = price - 20\\nendif\\nprint(price)\\n\\nHigh-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural language. BP3 and BP5 only to be given if sensible check for price being over the appropriate threshold. BP4 must check that price is both larger than 100 and not larger than 200; do not give mark for simply checking price is larger than 100. This may be implicit (e.g. using elseif).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and store price","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 200","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if true, reduce price by 40","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 100 and not > 200","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if true, reduce price by 20","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(b)","parent_question":18,"topic":"refine-stock-program","marks":5,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An item is classified as \\"In demand\\" if OCR Tech have between 5 and 25 inclusive in stock.\\n\\nA program is written that allows the user to input the current stock level and output whether the item is in demand or not."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"stocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel >= 5 or =< 25 then\\n  print(Not in demand)\\nelse\\n  print(In demand)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"The program contains syntax and logic errors.\\n\\nRefine the program to correct the errors and write the refined version of the program.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nstocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel >= 5 and stocklevel <= 25 then\\n  print(\\"In demand\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"Not in demand\\")\\nendif\\n\\nAlternative:\\nstocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel < 5 or stocklevel > 25 then\\n  print(\\"Not in demand\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"In demand\\")\\nendif\\n\\nHigh-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural language. As a matter of principle, a candidate who refines the program to work fully but in a different format should gain full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"checking both values (e.g. or changed to and if appropriate)","marks":1},{"criteria":"if statement in correct format (e.g. checking against stocklevel for each condition)","marks":1},{"criteria":"if statement uses correct comparisons (e.g. >= and <=)","marks":1},{"criteria":"print statements in correct position","marks":1},{"criteria":"print statements include string delimiters around both string outputs","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"19(a)","parent_question":19,"topic":"structure-diagram","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school uses a mobile phone app to allow parents to book appointments for parents\' evenings.\\n\\nParents must log in before they can use the system. They then choose to book a new appointment, view all appointments already made or update their personal details. If parents choose to view their appointments, they can either view them on-screen or print them off.\\n\\nA structure diagram has been used to design the mobile phone app.\\n\\nWrite one letter from the following table in each space to complete the structure diagram."},{"type":"table","headers":["Letter","Task"],"rows":[["A","Book new appointment"],["B","Check attendance of chi