has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is. Give benefit of the doubt where unclear. Examples: 'focus on student names and events'; 'ignore data such as students' favourite subjects'; 'store year groups instead of ages or DOB'; 'shows student IDs instead of full student details'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design), applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2eii","parent_question":2,"topic":"decomposition in program design","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description of what decomposition is. Give BOD where unclear. Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields (e.g. split into StudentID, YearGroup, etc). BOD if answer discusses one table but suggests other tables could be used. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program. Do not give answers relating simply to data being split into smaller groups unless this clearly relates to how data is decomposed into tables in the database. Examples: 'splits the program up into different events'; 'separates the validation routines into subroutines'; 'breaks the database down into a table per event'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables, applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2f","parent_question":2,"topic":"winning team algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score)."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"For BP3, allow 'stop' to be entered for either team name or score (or both). Allow a third input (e.g. 'do you wish to stop?'). Allow use of break (or equivalent) to exit loop for BP3. Allow use of recursive function(s) for BP2/3. Initialisation of variables not needed (assume variables are 0 or empty string if not set). Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score. BP4/5 could be done in many ways. Allow use of max/sum functions and arrays/lists. FT for BP6 if attempt made at calculating highest score/name. If the answer simply asks for multiple entries (not using iteration), BP2 and 3 cannot be accessed but all others available. For minor syntax errors (e.g. missing quotation marks, == for assignment, spaces in variable names) penalise once then FT. 'input' cannot be used as a variable name.\\n\\nExample 1:\\nhighscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nExample 2 (alternative):\\nscores = []\\nteams = []\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  scores.append(score)\\n  teams.append(team)\\nendwhile\\nhighscore = max(scores)\\nhighteam = teams[scores.index(highscore)]\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store / use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"...to enter team/score until 'stop' entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3i","parent_question":3,"topic":"binary search trace","marks":3,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Show how a binary search will be used to find the number 10 in the following data set:"},{"type":"text","value":"1   2   5   6   7   10   20"}],"answer":{"guidance":"BP1 can be given for generic answer. BP2 and 3 must be linked to the data set given. For BP2, must remove 1, 2, 5 and 6 from the list if discussing individual numbers. Allow FT for BP3 if this is done incorrectly.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare to / pick out middle value (which is 6)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Discard only left side / retain only right side (because 6 < 10)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Compare to / pick out (middle value which is) 10","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3ii","parent_question":3,"topic":"binary search prerequisite","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State one pre-requisite for a binary search algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"The data must already be in order for a binary search to function.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Data must be sorted / in order","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3iii","parent_question":3,"topic":"merge sort identification","marks":1,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box to identify the name of the sorting algorithm that splits data into individual items before recombining in order."},{"type":"table","headers":["Sorting algorithm","Tick one box"],"rows":[["Bubble sort",""],["Insertion sort",""],["Merge sort",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Merge sort is the divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge sort","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4a","parent_question":4,"topic":"linear search identification","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An array stores a collection of words. The array has the identifier data."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"data = [\\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\", \\"when\\", \\"and\\", \\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"A searching algorithm is used to find a word in the array data. The algorithm used to search for a word is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"word = input(\\"Enter a word to find\\")\\nfound = False\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == word then\\n    found = True\\n  endif\\nnext i\\nprint(found)"},{"type":"text","value":"State the name of this searching algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept Sequential Search.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Linear search","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4b","parent_question":4,"topic":"refining algorithm to count occurrences","marks":4,"command_word":"refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Some words appear in the array more than once."},{"type":"text","value":"Refine the algorithm to:\\n- count the number of times a word appears in the array\\n- output this number.\\nFor example, the algorithm should output 3 if the input is \\"and\\"."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow answers that completely rewrite the algorithm as long as the points on the left are met.\\n\\nExample answer:\\nsearch = input(\\"Enter a word\\")\\ncount = 0\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == search then\\n    count = count + 1\\nnext i\\nprint(count)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Declares variable to store the count (before the for loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Initialises variable to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"Increments variable by 1 each time the word is found","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs count value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4ci","parent_question":4,"topic":"insertion sort sorted and unsorted sections","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is used to put the array with the identifier \`data\` into alphabetical order. The diagram shows the array when the insertion sort is almost complete."},{"type":"table","headers":[],"variant":"separated-boxes","sections":[{"label":"Section A","span":6},{"label":"Section B","span":2}],"rows":[["and","and","and","it","or","when","or","it"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Section A: the first six items (\`and\`, \`and\`, \`and\`, \`it\`, \`or\`, \`when\`).\\nSection B: the final two items (\`or\`, \`it\`).\\n\\nExplain why the array is shown with two sections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"An insertion sort maintains a sorted partition at one end of the array and an unsorted partition at the other.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A is sorted","marks":1},{"criteria":"B is not (yet) sorted","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4cii","parent_question":4,"topic":"completing insertion sort steps","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps an insertion sort takes to complete sorting the array data."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 marks, 1 mark per point. Max two marks if candidate simply states to insert 'or' then 'it' into the correct place without discussing how this is determined. Max one if generic answer with no reference to given array. Allow answers referring to swapping items down the array to get to the correct position.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare 'or' with next item on left","marks":1},{"criteria":"'or' is smaller than 'when' so it repeats the comparison with next item and inserts 'or' / item in correct place, based on comparisons","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat to insert 'it'","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4d","parent_question":4,"topic":"merge sort identification","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"One sorting algorithm splits an array into single items and then combines them into an ordered array. State the name of this sorting algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other sorting algorithms that use divide and conquer.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge sort","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5a","parent_question":5,"topic":"purpose of temp variable in insertion sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is one type of sorting algorithm. A student has written a pseudocode algorithm to perform an insertion sort on a 1D array names."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"names = [\\"Kareem\\", \\"Sarah\\", \\"Zac\\", \\"Sundip\\", \\"Anika\\"]\\nfor count = 1 to names.length - 1\\n  pos = count\\n  while (pos > 0 and names[pos] < names[pos - 1])\\n    temp = names[pos]\\n    names[pos] = names[pos - 1]\\n    names[pos - 1] = temp\\n    pos = pos - 1\\n  endwhile\\nnext count"},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the purpose of the variable temp in the insertion sort pseudocode algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not allow answers that clearly refer to storing the position / index (or any other out-of-context data) for BP1; it is the name itself that is being stored, not the position. If unclear, allow BOD. E.g. do not allow 'holds the values of the index / holds value for position of the name'. Allow FT for subsequent points.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Stores/holds data/value/name/names[pos]","marks":1},{"criteria":"...so (value) can be changed / swapped / moved / overwritten / inserted / without being lost / will be assigned to names[pos-1]","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5b","parent_question":5,"topic":"condition-controlled inner loop","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort contains a nested loop; a loop within a loop. In this pseudocode algorithm the outer loop is a count-controlled loop and the inner loop is a condition-controlled loop."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the inner loop needs to be a condition-controlled loop."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 1 from each section, 2 marks total. Do not allow 'while names are in the wrong order'. BP4 must have reference to checking a condition / condition being met, not just having a condition.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reason (1 mark): do not know how many iterations / swaps needed, or do not know (at run time) how many times the value will change positions, or do not know how many times a condition-controlled loop will need to run / execute","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description (1 mark): condition-controlled loops run while/until a condition is true/false/met; repeats while value in [pos-1] is larger than value in [pos] / while further swap needed; will swap value until in correct position; more efficient than / does not need to iterate as many times as a count-controlled / for loop","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5ci","parent_question":5,"topic":"difference between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A bubble sort is a type of sorting algorithm."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one difference between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for insertion and bubble sort, max 2. Answer must reference both bubble sort and insertion sort for 2 marks (except if efficiency mark plus expansion given). Allow reference to Big O for efficiency discussion. Only award efficiency once. Only award fewer iterations once. Do not accept 'completes in one iteration' for insertion sort. Accept list / data / values / etc for array. 'When data more scrambled' only makes sense when discussing efficiency/speed; do not give marks for saying that either can handle data that is more scrambled. Do not accept 'bubble/insertion sort does not' for 2nd mark.\\n\\nInsertion sort points: inserts/moves values into correct position; inserts value once (then in correct position); stops when end of array reached / completes in one pass through the array; moves items down the array / left; start of array becomes sorted first; creates a sorted array within an array / has a sorted/unsorted partition; starts on 2nd value; more efficient/faster than bubble sort; ...because fewer iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.\\n\\nBubble sort points: compares/swaps pairs of values; value is repeatedly moved/swapped (until in correct position); repeats if a swap has been made / needs multiple passes; will complete a final iteration once sorted (to check for no swaps needed); moves items up the array; end of array becomes sorted first; moves/bubbles the highest value to the top; less efficient/slower than insertion sort (on large sets of values); ...more iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid point describing insertion sort behaviour","marks":1},{"criteria":"One valid point describing bubble sort behaviour (contrasting)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5cii","parent_question":5,"topic":"similarities between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two similarities between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Allow reference to both sorting / putting items into order for BP1. 'Allows sorting of numbers and strings' meets BP1. Allow answers relating to not needing additional memory as BP2. Allow 'breaking into smaller lists' as divide-and-conquer for BP2. If answer is a statement (e.g. 'uses loops'), assume candidate is talking about both algorithms doing this.\\n\\nValid similarities include: both produce a sorted list/array; both work in place / without duplicating data / without using divide-and-conquer; both need a temporary variable; both swap values; both use loops/iteration/repeats; both loops are nested/inside each other; both (may) need multiple passes; both use selection; both work with an array/list data structure; both work from left to right; both build up sorted list one item at a time (after every pass); both compare (pairs of) values; both (typically) less efficient/slower than merge sort; both inefficient/slow for larger/unsorted lists / efficient for small/(nearly) sorted lists; both start by comparing first two values.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid similarity","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid similarity","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6a","parent_question":6,"topic":"merge sort of positive and negative numbers","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program stores the following list of positive and negative numbers. The numbers need sorting into ascending order using a merge sort."},{"type":"text","value":"45   12   -99   100   -13   0   17   -27"},{"type":"text","value":"The first step is to divide the list into individual lists of one number each. This has been done for you."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the merge sort of the data by showing each step of the process."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not credit BP3 simply for a sorted list. Groups of numbers must clearly be the correct size. Do not allow answers that show lists being merged and then sorting in place; this is incorrect.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted lists of size 2: (12 45) (-99 100) (-13 0) (-27 17)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted lists of size 4: (-99 12 45 100) (-27 -13 0 17)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted list of size 8: (-99 -27 -13 0 12 17 45 100)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6b","parent_question":6,"topic":"binary search description","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Once a list of numbers are in order, a binary search can be run on the data."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps a binary search will follow to look for a number in a sorted list."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any four bullet points for 1 mark each. Do not allow 'split the list in half' on its own as first step; this is incorrect. Can get BP1 and BP2 in one step (e.g. 'check if the middle number is the one we're 