on algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated. The height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \\"VALID\\" or \\"NOT VALID\\" depending on the height input."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (checking for valid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nExample 2 (checking for invalid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nAnswers using AND/OR must be logically correct, e.g. if height >=40 and height <=180. Do not accept if height >=40 and <=180. BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3. Ignore conversion to int on input. input cannot be used as a variable name. Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for HLL/ERL. Do not accept => or >.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 // < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 // > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"test data (normal, boundary, erroneous)","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests. Complete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"No need to include decimals, e.g. accept 50. Ignore cm if given. Answer must be actual data (e.g. 50) and not description of data (e.g. \\"a value between 40 and 180\\"). If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for erroneous.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(d)","parent_question":2,"topic":"SQL","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database. The database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants",""],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","20.3"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.2"],["","","","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1. Spelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(e)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"abstraction","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is. Give BOD where this is unclear. Examples: \\"focus on student names and events\\"; \\"ignore data such as students\' favourite subjects\\"; \\"store year groups instead of ages or DOB\\"; \\"shows student IDs instead of full student details\\".","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design), applied to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(e)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"decomposition","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description of what decomposition is. Give BOD where this is unclear. Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields (e.g. split into StudentID, YearGroup, etc). Examples: \\"splits the program up into different events\\"; \\"separates the validation routines into subroutines\\"; \\"breaks the database down into a table per event\\".","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables, applied to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(f)","parent_question":2,"topic":"iteration algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score)."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1:\\nhighscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nFor BP3, allow \\"stop\\" to be entered for either team name or score (or both). Allow use of break (or equivalent) to exit loop for BP3. Allow use of recursive function(s) for BP2/3. Initialisation of variables not needed. Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score. BP4/5 could be done in many ways. Allow any logically valid method. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists. FT for BP6 if attempt made at calculating highest score/name. If answer simply asks for multiple entries (not using iteration), BP2 and 3 cannot be accessed but all others available. input cannot be used as a variable name.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store / use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"To enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)","parent_question":3,"topic":"types of errors (syntax)","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State what is meant by the term syntax error. Give one example of a syntax error in a program."},{"type":"text","value":"Give your answer in the form:\\nDefinition: <state what is meant by a syntax error>\\nExample: <give one example of a syntax error in a program>"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 1 mark for definition that is clearly different from a logic error. BOD code/program etc for definition. Do not allow answers linked to data types. \\"incorrect grammar\\" by itself is NE. Do not allow \\"stop working\\", \\"does not work\\", etc (TV). Do not accept missing quotation marks e.g. print(hello) (could be a variable name). BOD given code that could cause a syntax error in a high-level language.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition: (an error that) breaks the rules/grammar of the programming language // Stops the program from running // does not allow program to run // crashes the program // does not allow program to translate","marks":1},{"criteria":"Suitable example, e.g. misspelling key word (e.g. printt instead of print); missing/extra symbol (e.g. missing bracket, missing semicolon); mismatched quotes; invalid variable or function names; incorrect use of operators; use of reserved keywords for variables; incorrect capitalisation of keywords; incorrect indentation; missing concatenation","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"types of errors (logic)","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A student writes an algorithm to input two numbers and add them together to create a total. If the total is between 10 and 20 inclusive, \\"success\\" is output. If the total is not between 10 and 20 inclusive, \\"warning\\" is output."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 num1 = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\n02 num2 = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\n03 total = num1 + num1\\n04 if total >= 10 then\\n05   print(\\"success\\")\\n06 else\\n07   print(\\"warning\\")\\n08 endif"},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm does not work correctly."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the line number of the **two** logic errors in the algorithm and refine the code to correct each logic error."},{"type":"text","value":"Line number  ______"},{"type":"text","value":"Correction  ____________________________________________________________"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number  ______"},{"type":"text","value":"Correction  ____________________________________________________________"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each. Allow other logical corrections that will fix the problem identified and does not introduce any further errors. Allow descriptions of changes as long as clear exactly what will change. Do not allow ambiguous descriptions of changes to code. Ignore missing then from line 04. Ignore capitalisation. Allow other logical equivalent code e.g. total = int(num1) + int(num2); if 10 <= total <= 20.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 03 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Corrected line 03: total = num1 + num2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Line 04 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Corrected line 04: if total >= 10 and total <= 20 then","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"test plan (normal, boundary, erroneous)","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Drones flies goods around the country using drones. A pilot\'s flying experience is validated. An algorithm checks that the experience is between 0 and 20 years."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"exp = input(\\"Enter number of years\\")\\nif exp >= 0 and exp <= 20 then\\n  print(True)\\nelse\\n  print(False)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete this test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Experience in years","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["____","Normal","True"],["20","Boundary","____"],["32","____","____"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark per bullet point.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal row test data: Any value between 0 and 20 (e.g. 4)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row with 20: Boundary, expected output True","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row with 32: Erroneous / Invalid (or sensible alternative), expected output False","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary row test data: suitable value (e.g. 0 or 20)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"types of errors (syntax, logic)","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This pseudocode algorithm totals all the numbers in the 0-indexed array `scores`"},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"01 total = 0\\n02 for scoreCount = 1 to scores.length - 1\\n03   scores[scoreCount] = total + total\\n04 next scoreCount\\n05 print(total)"},{"type":"text","value":"The function `length` returns the number of elements in the array."},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm contains several errors."},{"type":"text","value":"Two types of errors in a program are syntax and logic errors."},{"type":"text","value":"State what is meant by a syntax error and a logic error."},{"type":"text","value":"Syntax error: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Logic error: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each. Question asks for a definition. Examples may strengthen the response but are not acceptable by themselves. Do not allow \\"error/problem in the code, does not work / does not do what designed/intended to do\\" for either, this applies to both. \\"Error in the syntax\\" or \\"error in the logic\\" are NE even with examples.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Syntax error: Error in the rules/grammar (of the program language) / Program does not (fully) run / translate / execute / start (BOD)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Logic error: Produces incorrect / unexpected result/output / Program runs/does not crash","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"types of errors (logic)","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This pseudocode algorithm totals all the numbers in the 0-indexed array scores."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 total = 0\\n02 for scoreCount = 1 to scores.length - 1\\n03   scores[scoreCount] = total + total\\n04 next scoreCount\\n05 print(total)"},{"type":"text","value":"The function length returns the number of elements in the array. The algorithm contains several errors. Two types of errors in a program are syntax and logic errors."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify two logic errors in the pseudocode algorithm. Write the refined line to correct each error."},{"type":"text","value":"**Error 1**"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Corrected line: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"**Error 2**"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Corrected line: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each line number correctly identified. 1 mark for each correction. Correction must match line number. If wrong line number, do not mark correction. If no line number, mark correction only. Do not penalise if response removes -1 from scores.length as long as it starts at 0. Do not penalise potential off by 1 errors for looping (Python). Do not penalise case or minor spelling errors as long as intention is clear. Allow description of change that would be made (e.g. \\"change 1 to 0\\"). First correction is fixing indexing error so element 0 is included. Second correction is fixing addition of total. If both errors fixed on line 03, full marks should be given. e.g. total = total + scores[scoreCount-1].","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line number 02 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correction: for scoreCount = 0 to scores.length - 1","marks":1},{"criteria":"Line number 03 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correction: total = total + scores[scoreCount] (or total = scores[scoreCount] + total, or total += scores[scoreCount])","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(i)","parent_question":6,"topic":"purpose of testing","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Charlie is developing an adding game. The rules of the game are:\\n- the player is asked 3 addition questions\\n- each question asks the player to add together two random whole numbers between 1 and 10 inclusive\\n- if the player gets the correct answer, 1 is added to their score\\n- at the end of the game their score is displayed."},{"type":"text","value":"Charlie has been told that the game will need to be tested before giving it to the players."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why programs should be tested before use."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Allow answers that explain what would happen if not tested (e.g. \\"there might be bugs\\"). Valid points: check the program works (as intended); meets user requirements; gives the correct output / result; find / detect / check for errors / bugs; check the program does not crash / is robust / executes / runs; to try and break the program / destructive testing; test for / improve usability / user experience / performance / check user feedback is suitable; allow any errors to be fixed / make changes / improvements as a result of testing; ensure no problems / issues fixed when released; defensive design considerations / anticipating misuse / so cannot be misused.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid reason for testing","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid reason for testing","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(ii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"types of testing (iterative, final/terminal)","marks":2,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by naming and describing one type of test that should be used on Charlie\'s program before releasing it."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test type","Description"],"rows":[["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for name, 1 mark for matching description. Allow other sensible descriptive names for testing. Description must match test type. Must be a description and not just an example, but example may support description. Do not accept descriptions that simply repeat type of test without further clarification. Allow black box testing / white box testing / other sensible valid types. Do not accept examples of validation. Examples: Final / terminal testing - Completed at the end of development / before release / to test the product as a whole. Iterative / incremental testing - Completed during development / after each module is completed / test module in isolation. Normal testing - test using data that should be accepted. Boundary / Extreme testing - test using data that is on the edge of being acceptable. Invalid / Erroneous testing - test using data that should be rejected.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correctly names a type of test","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of that test type","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(iii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"IDE features for testing","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by identifying and describing two features of an IDE that can be used when testing a program."},{"type":"table","headers":["Feature","Description"],"rows":[["",""],["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for feature, 1 mark for matching description. Allow other sensible names for features. Description must add more than is given in the identification of the feature. Description must match feature. \\"finding errors\\" is NE for description of error reporting. Examples: Translator / compiler / interpreter - convert to low-level/machine code / allow program to be executed / produce executable file. Run-time environment / output window - allows program / code to be run / shows output. Error reporting / diagnostics - identify location/detail of errors / suggests fixes. Debugger - find errors. Stepping - execute/run the program line by line. Variable watch - see the contents/data held in variables. Break points - will allow the program to stop at a chosen / set position. Text/code editor - allows program code to be written/changed. Pretty printing / keyword highlighting - allows keywords / variables to be coloured / identified. Keyword completion / syntax suggestion - suggests code/syntax when first part entered.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First IDE feature correctly identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of first feature","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second IDE feature correctly identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of second feature","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_question":7,"topic":"data types","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Security Services is a company that installs intruder alarm systems in commercial buildings. The systems use a computer that is connected to the door sensors and window sensors. The following data is stored in the system:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Data stored","Variable identifier","Example data"],"rows":[["The user\'s name","UserName","Admin123"],["A telephone number to call when the alarm is activated","EmergencyPhoneNumber","+449999999999"],["Whether a door sensor is activated","DoorSensorActive","True"],["Whether a window sensor is activated","WindowSensorActive","True"],["A timer that counts, to the nearest second, how long a door sensor has been activated","DoorActiveTime","100"],["A timer that counts, to the nearest second, how long a window sensor has been activated","WindowActiveTime","100"],["Whether the system is armed","SystemArmed","True"],["Whether the system is in test mode","TestModeActive","True"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Below is a table showing some variables within the program. Tick one box in each row to identify the most appropriate data type for each variable."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Boolean","Char","String","Integer","Real"],"rows":[["UserName","","","","",""],["EmergencyPhoneNumber","","","","",""],["DoorSensorActive","","","","",""],["DoorActiveTime","","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each row. No mark if more than 1 tick on a row. Allow other indications of choice (e.g. cross) as long as clear.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"UserName: String","marks":1},{"criteria":"EmergencyPhoneNumber: String","marks":1},{"criteria":"DoorSensorActive: Boolean","marks":1},{"criteria":"DoorActiveTime: Integer","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(i)","parent_question":8,"topic":"casting","marks":2,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Each member of staff that works in a restaurant is given a Staff ID. This is calculated using the following algorithm."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 surname = input(\\"Enter surname\\")\\n02 year = input(\\"Enter starting year\\")\\n03 staffID = surname + str(year)\\n04 while staffID.length < 10\\n05   staffID = staffID + \\"x\\"\\n06 endwhile\\n07 print(\\"ID \\" + staffID)"},{"type":"text","value":"Define the term casting and give the line number where casting has been used in the algorithm."},{"type":"text","value":"Definition  ................................................................................................................................................"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number  ......................................................................................................................"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not accept \\"change to string\\" - this is the use in this example but not a definition.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition: Convert/change one data type to another","marks":1},{"criteria":"Line 03 // 3 // three","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(ii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"trace table","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following trace table for the given algorithm when the surname \\"Kofi\\" and the year 2021 are entered. You may not need to use all rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","surname","year","staffID","Output"],"rows":[["01","Kofi","","",""],["02","","2021","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 2 if incorrect order. Ignore misspelling of Kofi. Penalise lack of / errors with line numbers once then FT. Ignore capitalisation. Ignore additional lines unless outcome impacted. staffID does not have space in. Output does have a space in. Penalise spaces once then FT. Quotes around answer is OK, but do not allow quotes around partial answers.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Kofi2021 as staffID on line 03","marks":1},{"criteria":"Kofi2021x as staffID on line 05","marks":1},{"criteria":"Kofi2021xx as staffID on line 05","marks":1},{"criteria":"ID Kofi2021xx output on line 07 as first and only output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(i)","parent_question":9,"topic":"validation algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Customers at a hotel can stay between 1 and 5 (inclusive) nights and can choose between a basic room or a premium room."},{"type":"text","value":"When a new booking is recorded, the details are entered into a program to validate the values. The following criteria are checked:\\n- `firstName` and `surname` are not empty\\n- `room` is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"\\n- `nights` is between 1 and 5 (inclusive).\\nIf any invalid data is found \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\nIf all data is valid \\"ALLOWED\\" is displayed."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following program to validate the inputs."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"pseudocode","value":"firstName = input(\\"Enter a first name\\")\\n\\nsurname = input(\\"Enter a surname\\")\\n\\nroom = input(\\"Enter basic or premium\\")\\n\\nnights = input(\\"Enter between 1 and 5 nights\\")\\n\\nstayComplete = False"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must have some attempt at all three checks to give output mark(s). Check for nights must check both upper and lower limits. Iteration can be used as validation if input repeatedly asked for until valid answer given. Do not accept logically incorrect Boolean conditions such as if firstname or surname == \\"\\". Do not accept descriptive symbols for >=, <=. Ignore capitalisation. Example:\\nvalid = True\\nif firstname == \\"\\" or surname == \\"\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif room != \\"basic\\" and room != \\"premium\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif nights < 1 or nights > 5 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif valid then\\n  print(\\"ALLOWED\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"NOT ALLOWED\\")\\nendif\\nBP1 to 3 can check for valid or invalid inputs. Pay particular attention to use of AND / OR. Only give marks for output if these work together correctly.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks that both firstname and surname are not empty","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that room is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that nights is between 1 and 5 (inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) if any of the 3 checks are invalid (must check all three)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) only if all three checks are valid (must check all three)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(ii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"test plan (normal, boundary, erroneous)","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan to check whether the number of nights is validated correctly."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (number of nights)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["2","","ALLOWED"],["","Boundary","ALLOWED"],["","Erroneous / Invalid","NOT ALLOWED"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other descriptions that mean normal (e.g. valid / typical / acceptable).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First row: Normal","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary row test data: 1 or 5 (not 0 or 6 as says allowed)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous row test data: Any numeric value except 1 to 5 // any non-numeric input (e.g. \\"bananas\\")","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(i)","parent_question":10,"topic":"validation algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven."},{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership only sells cars that have fewer than 10 000 miles and are 5 years old or less."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that will:\\n- ask the user to enter the number of miles and the age of a car\\n- validate the input to check that only sensible values that are in the given range are entered\\n- output True if valid data has been entered or False if invalid data has been entered."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet, max 4 for BP1-4; BP5 for outputs. BP2 and 3 must check for both ends of range - must check that input data is not negative. Allow FT for BP4 if already penalised under BP2 and/or 3 and output is otherwise correct. Example:\\nmiles = input(\\"enter miles driven\\")\\nage = input(\\"enter age of car\\")\\nvalid = True\\nif miles > 10000 or miles < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nelseif age > 5 or age < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nprint(valid)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Miles and age input separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid mileage","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid age","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks both are greater than / greater than equal to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly outputs both True and False","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(ii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"test data (normal, erroneous, boundary)","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The validation routine from part (i) must be tested with normal, erroneous and boundary test data. Identify suitable test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Miles","Age"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Erroneous","",""],["Boundary","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per row, max 3. Specific data must be given, not a description. e.g. Normal: 7,000 and 3; Erroneous: 12,000 and 7; Boundary: 10,000 and 5.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: miles (0-9,999), age (0-5)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: miles (less than 0, larger than 9,999), age (less than 0 / more than 5) // non-numeric data","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: miles (-1/0/9,999/10,000), age (-1/0/5/6)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(iii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"types of testing (iterative)","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify when iterative testing is performed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept any sensible answer indicating testing during development.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"During development // whilst writing the program // before development is complete","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11(a)(i)","parent_question":11,"topic":"test plan (normal, boundary)","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Tech is an online shop that sells electronics such as TVs and game consoles. The following flowchart shows an algorithm to calculate the price of an item during a sale period."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/sale-price-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Sale-price flowchart that reduces prices over 200 by 40 and prices over 100 by 20 before output."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Price input","Test type","Expected price output"],"rows":[["50","Normal",""],["100","Boundary",""],["150","Normal",""],["200","Boundary",""],["250","Normal",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark if two correct, two marks if four correct, three marks if all correct. Expected outputs: 50 -> 50, 100 -> 100, 150 -> 130, 200 -> 180, 250 -> 210.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Two correct expected outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"Four correct expected outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"All five correct expected outputs (50, 100, 130, 180, 210)","marks":1}